2701:
** κατατρέχω**,
_aorist 1_ _infinitive_ καταθρέξαι only in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}: _aorist 2_ [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|κατέδρᾰμον Aristophanes Comicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Ecclesiazusae” 961}}], etc.: _perfect_ -δεδράμηκα [ᾰμ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “HG” 4.7.6:}} —_passive_, _aorist_ _infinitive_ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|καταδρᾰμηθῆναι 4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 1.21}}:—**run down**, Ar.[same place]; [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|ἀπὸ τῶν ἄκρων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.192; κάτω [prev\. author] 3156; ἐπὶ θάλατταν Xenophon Historicus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Anabasis” 7.1.20; ἐπί τινας “Act.Ap.” 21.32}}].
**2** of seamen or passengers by sea, **run to land, disembark**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “HG” 5.1.12; εἰς ἐμπόρια 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.91.2}}: _metaphorically_, κ. ξένιον ἄστυ **come to a haven in**.., _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.23.}}
**II** _transitive_, **run down, inveigh against**, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|τὴν Σπάρτην Plato Philosophus [{5-6th c.BC}] “Leges” 806c, compare 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 12, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 50.2, etc.: more frequently with _genitive_, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Vit.” p.42 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, etc.; κ. τῶν μάντεων 3rd c.AD: Diogenes Laertius 2.135; τῶν συνόντων τοῖς δυνάσταις Dio Cassius Historicus [{2-3rd c.AD}] 61.10; τῆς μέθης 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.1c e; Ἀλκιβιάδου ὡς οἰνόφλυγος [prev\. author] 5.22o c, compare 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “Synt.” 100.19; κατὰ τῆς βουλῆς, κατὰ τῆς μοναρχίας, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 36.44, 66.13.}}]
**II.2** **overrun, ravage, lay waste**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|τῆς Σαλαμῖνος τὰ πολλά 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.94, compare 8.92, Dionysius Epigrammaticus Comedy texts 3.5, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.44, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 2}}, etc., **oppress**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|τοὺς γεωργούς “PTeb.” 41.30 (2nd c.BC).}}
**II.3** **run over**, with _genitive_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|κὰδ δ᾽ ἄρα οἱ βλεφάρων βαρὺς ἔδραμεν ὕπνος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.204}}.
**II.4** **pursue**, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.26.37.}}
**II.5** **hurry**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.512e.}}
**II.6** **slip down**, of a bandage, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(1).829.}}

2702:
** καταφέρω**,
_future_ κατοίσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 28, -οίσομαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.425}}: _aorist 1_ κατήνεγκα {{LXX|LXX.Gen.37.2}}, _infinitive_ - ενεγκεῖν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.62.9}}; _Doric dialect_ κατέφειρα {{Refs|“GDI” 2317.8 (Delph.)}}:—**bring down**, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, οὗ μ᾽ ἄχος ὀξὺ κατοίσεται Ἄϊδος εἴσω}} **will bring** me **down** to the grave, Il.[same place]; βαρυπεσῆ καταφέρω ποδὸς ἀκμάν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 370 (Lyric poetry)}}; of rivers, κ. χρυσίον, γῆν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 833b17, “Pr.” 935a16: Comedy texts, ὁ Κρᾶθις ἡμῖν κ. μάζας 5th c.BC: Metagenes Comicus 6.1}}; especially of cutting instruments, κ. τὴν σμινύην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 11.32}}; τὴν δίκελλαν, τὴν σφῦραν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 7, “Prom.” 2: with _dative_ object, κ. τὸ ξίφος τῷ πολεμίῳ}} **let** it **fall upon** him, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.236e: with _genitive_, τὴν ἅρπην τῆς ἰξύος 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 1.3; τῶν γνάθων τὸ ξυρόν 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.66: _metaphorically_, ψόγον τινός LXX.Gen.37.2}}: _absolutely_, **hew downwards, deal a blow**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 8, “Somn.” 3; κ. πληγήν [prev. author] “Tim.” 40, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.69 (but also κατήνεγκε πληγαῖς τὴν κεφαλήν “PTeb.” 138 (2nd c.BC)).}}
**b** **pull down, demolish**, πύργους {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.64.11}}; ἥλους (warts) {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 10.4.}}
**c** **pay down, discharge**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1348a2, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.62.9, 33.13.6, “GDI” 1754 (Delph.), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 28.}}
**d** **pass, evacuate**, τὰ σπλάγχνα μετὰ τῶν σιτίων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 5.9.4.}}
**e** **refer** a thing, ἀπό τινος ἐφ᾽ ἕτερον, variant for{μεταφέρειν}, Legal cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.94.}}
**f** **carry down**, in reckoning, etc., πλῆθος ἀμήχανον ἐτῶν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 18; τὸ τῆς εὐδαιμονίας εἰς τὰ ζῷα 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.4.1.}}
**2** _passive_, **to be brought down** by a river, of gold dust, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.93}}; from an upper story, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.63}}; **to move downwards with violence, to be discharged**, of humours, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.8.18}}; **to be couched**, of a cataract, - ενεχθέντος τοῦ ὑποχύματος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.89.}}
**2.b** **descend, sink**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 590b8}}; κ. ὁ ἥλιος, ἡ σελήνη, ἡ ἡμέρα,{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 552b21, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 21, “Tim.” 12}}; κ. ὁ λύχνος **is near going out**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Caes.” 69}}; κ. [ἡ ἄμπελος] **is perishing**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.13.5}}; of dancers, κ. ἐπὶ γῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 36 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, compare 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 228}}; of a sick person, κ. καθάπερ νεκρόν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.591}}; but ἐπὶ πόδας, of a patient in bed, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18(2).60.}}
**2.c** **fall, flow down**, of rain or rivers, [{{Refs 5th c.AD+|“Geoponica” 5.2.16, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {Πεντέλεια}.}}]
**2.d** **tumble down**, αἱ οἰκίαι κ. ἐπί τινα.. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Dio” 44; ἀφ᾽ ὕψους -ενεχθεῖσα γυνή 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.84.}}
**2.e** **to be weighed down**, ἐν τοῖσιν ὕπνοισι variant in {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.45, compare 5.50; κ. καὶ νυστάζειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Somno et Vigilia” 456b31; ἐς ὕπνον 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 2.4; ὕπνῳ βαθεῖ NT.Act.20.9, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “de gymnastica” 54; ὑπὸ μέθης 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.461c}}: _absolutely_, **drop asleep**, opposed to ἐγείρεσθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 779a9, “Insomn.” 462a10}}; **to be semi-comatose**, ἀγρυπνεῖν τε ἅμα καὶ -εσθαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.497.}}
**II** **carry back, carry home**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 955.}}
**II.2** of a storm, **drive to land**, ὁ χειμὼν κατήνεγκε τὰς ναῦς ἐς τὴν Πύλον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.3, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.24.11}}:— _passive_, καταφέρεται χειμῶνι ἐς τὸ Ἀθηναίων στρατόπεδον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137, compare 3.69}}: generally, in _passive_, **to be landed, discharged**, of cargoes, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 278ii 13 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**III** _passive_, _metaphorically_, **to be brought** to a point, ἐπὶ γνώμην, ἐλπίδα, etc., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.19.13, 6.9.3, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.6.1; ἐπὶ τὰς αὐτὰς διανοίας 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Lysia” 17, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 29, +others: _absolutely_ (compare καταφορά 11.3), [prev. work] 30}}:—also _active_, **have recourse**, ἐπ᾽ οὐθὲν ψεῦδος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1.159 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**III.2** **tend**, ἡ [σύνταξις] ἐπὶ τὸ προστακτικὸν φύσει κ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 232.8; τῶν ῥημάτων -φερομένων εἰς τὴν ἐπὶ τέλους βαρεῖαν [prev. work] 134.25.}}
**III.3** **enter the lists**, like Latin _descendere in arenam_, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 59.67.}}
**IV** **bring against**, τὴν διαβολὴν κ. τινός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1437a19.}}
**V** _intransitive_ in _active_, **to be prone, inclined**, κ. εἰς τὰς γυναῖκας {{Refs|“POxy.” 465.146.}}

2703:
** κατα-φεύγω**,
_future_ - φεύξομαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.41}}:—**flee for refuge**, ἐς τὸ [ἱρόν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.113, compare 1.145; ἐπὶ Διὸς βωμόν [prev. author] 5.46: with _accusative_, οὐκ ἔχω βωμὸν κ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 911 (troch.)}}; -πεφευγέναι ἐν τόπῳ **flee and take refuge** in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 260c, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.5.5}}; ἐκεῖ, ἐνθάδε κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.71, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.28}}; ὅποι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.8.10}}; κ. εἴς τινα **flee for protection** to him, ὃς ἂν φεύγων καταφύγῃ ἐς τούτους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23; εἰς ὑμᾶς κ. καὶ ἀντιβολῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.149; ἐπί τινα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.19, etc.; πρὸς ὑμᾶς [prev. author] 8.41; παρ᾽ ἡμῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.194.}}
**2** ἐκ τῆς μάχης κ. **escape** from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.75: _absolutely_, ἄνω μάλ᾽ εἶσι καταφυγών (i.e. ὁ ἀτμός) 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 124.17.}}
**3** **have recourse**, εἰς ἔλεον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.2; εἰς σωτηρίαν [prev. author] 2.4.1; εἰς τοὺς λόγους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 99e, compare 76e; εἰς ὅρκον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1432a38; ἐπὶ τὰς μηχανάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 425d; ἐπὶ τὸν δικαστήν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1132a20}}; ἐπὶ τὸν λόγον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1105b13; ἐπὶ Καρχηδονίους 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.10.1, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 7; πρὸς θεῶν εὐχάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 244e; ὥς τινας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 24.10.11: with _dative_, τῇ μητρί 5th-6th c.BC: Ctesias Historicus “fragment” 29.57.}}
**4** εἰς τὴν τοῦ βίου μετριότητα κ. **fall back** upon, **appeal** to.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.76}}; ἐπὶ τὸ φάσκειν.. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.8.}}

2704:
** καταφθείρω**,
_future_ - φθερῶ {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.24o d}}:—**destroy, bring to naught**, στρατόν, πόλιν, ἔθνη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 345, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 331, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 697d, compare 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 159; ἔργα νομήων 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.122; κατέφθαρται ὄλβος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 251}}; ἐπεὶ δέ χ᾽ εἵκω οἴκαδις καταφθερείς **in sorry plight**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 35.13; - φθαρεὶς τὸν βίον 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 429, compare “Raccolta Lumbroso” 17 (3rd c.BC)}}; δένδρα -εφθαρμένα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 9(2).1109.74 (Coropa, 2nd c.BC),compare “PMagd.” 11.9 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**2** **corrupt**, τινα Plu.[same place]

2706:
** καταφρον-έω**,
**look down upon, think slightly of**, τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.63, etc.; τῶν παρόντων καταφρονῶν, τῶν ἀπόντων ἐπιθυμῶν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.78; κ. τοῦ κινδύνου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28c; τῆς τέχνης [prev. author] “Grg.” 512c, +others; καύματος καὶ ψύχους 4th c.BC: Ephorus Historicus 149 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; κυνηγεσίων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 1.18; θεῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 199, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 262; τῶν πτωχῶν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 301.10; οὐ δεῖ διαβολῆς κ. [prev. author] 88.1.}}
**2** with _accusative_, **regard slightly, despise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 503; τοὺς ἐπιόντας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34}}:—_passive_, **to be despised**, ὑπό τινων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 556d; εἰς τὰ πολεμικὰ καταφρονούμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.4.30}}: _future_ - φρονηθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.95, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.176: also in middle form - φρονήσομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 281c}}: _aorist_ -εφρονήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.108, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 273d.}}
**3** _absolutely_, **to be disdainful, deal contemptuously**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11, 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 1.3, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1378b15}}; τὸ -φρονοῦν **contempt**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.44.}}
**4** with _infinitive_, **think contemptuously that.., presume**, καταφρονήσαντες Ἀρκάδων κρέσσονες εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.66; καταφρονοῦντες κἂν προαισθέσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.83: also with _accusative_, - φρονήσαντες ταῦτα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.10.}}
**5** with _accusative_ et _genitive_, like{καταγιγνώσκω}, κ. τῶν Ἀθηναίων ἀδυνασίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.8.}}
**II** c._accusative_ _of things_, **fix one's thoughts upon, aim at**, τὴν τυραννίδα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59}}; τοὺς βύστακας μὴ καταφρόνει **do** not **think of** your moustache, **do** not **aim at having** one (because the Spartans had to shave the upper lip, compare μύσταξ), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 44.}}
**III** **come to one's senses**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἱερῆς νούσου” 15, “Nat.Mul.” 3 (probable reading), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.165f.}}

2708:
** καταχέω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.496 (tmesis), +others:}}_aorist 1_ κατέχεα, _Epic dialect_ and Lyric poetry κατέχευα (see. below):—_middle_, _Epic dialect_ _aorist 1_ κατεχεύατο {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hecale” 1.1.11}}; _infinitive_ - χέασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.50:}} —_passive_, _perfect_ κατακέχυμαι Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.140 (tmesis)}}: _aorist_ -εχύθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 854 (Lyric poetry): _Epic dialect_}}_aorist_ _passive_ (frequently in _tmesis_) κατέχυτο, κατέχυντο, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.282, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.411, “hymnus ad Venerem” 228}}:—**pour down upon, pour over**, with _dative_, κὰδ δέ οἱ ὕδωρ χεῦαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.435; so ἥ ῥά οἱ ἀχλὺν θεσπεσίην κατέχευε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.42; ὄρεος κορυφῇσι Νότος κατέχευεν ὀμίχλην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.10; τῷ γε χάριν κατέχευεν᾽ Ἀθήνη 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.12, etc.; σφιν.. πλοῦτον κατέχευε Κρονίων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.670; μὴ σφῶϊν ἐλεγχείην καταχεύῃ 23.408, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.38; οἷ.. κατ᾽ αἶσχος ἔχευε 11.433; ἐμῇ κεφαλῇ κατ᾽ ὀνείδεα χεῦαν 22.463; νεφέλαν κρατὶ κατέχευας 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.8}}; ἀντιπάλοις φόνον epigram cited in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marcellus” 30}}:—_passive_, κὰδ δ᾽ ἄχος οἱ χύτο ὀφθαλμοῖσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.282}}; κατὰ.. ὀρόφοισιν αἷμα.. κέχυται Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} [same place]; δάκρυσι βλέφαρα-χυθέντα E.[same place]; οἱ -χυθέντες {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 3.7.29}}:—also _active_ with _genitive_, rarely in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ὅς σφωϊν.. ἔλαιον χαιτάων κατέχευε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.282, compare 765: frequently later, καταχέουσι αἷμα τοῦ ἀκινάκεος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.62; κατάχει σὺ τῆς χορδῆς τὸ μέλι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1040}}; ἔτνος τοὐλατῆρος{{Refs 7th c.BC+|[prev. work] 246; τοῦ δήμου καταχεῖν.. πλουθυγίειαν [prev. author] “Eq.” 1091; ἵππερόν μου κατέχεεν τῶν χρημάτων [prev. author] “Nu.” 74, compare “Pl.” 790; βλασφημίαν τῶν ἱερῶν κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 8o od; also κὰδ δὲ χευάτω μύρον.. κὰτ τὼ στήθεος 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 36, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 398a:}} —_middle_, κατὰ τῶν ἱματίων καταχεόμενοι [ἄκρατον] **letting** it **be poured over**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 637e}}:— _passive_, κατὰ τοῖν κόραιν ὕπνου τι καταχεῖται γλυκύ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 7.}}
**2**simply, **pour, shower down**, χιόνα, νιφάδας ἐπὶ χθονί, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.206, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.158; ψιάδας κ. ἔραζε 16.459; so κατὰ δ᾽ ἠέρα πουλὺν ἔχευεν 8.50; κατὰ δ᾽ ὕπνον ἔχευεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.245:}} —_middle_, νότος.. χύσιν κατεχεύατο φύλλων Call.[same place]:—_passive_, ἴδρως κακχέεται {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.13.}}
**2.b** **throw, cast down**, θύσθλα χαμαὶ κατέχευαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.134; κατὰ δ᾽ ἡνία χεῦεν ἔραζε 17.619}}; ὅπλα τε πάντα εἰς ἄντλον κατέχυνθ᾽ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.411}}; πέπλον μὲν.. κατέχευεν ἐπ᾽ οὔδει **let** the robe **fall upon** the floor, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.734; τεῖχος.. εἰς ἅλα πᾶν κ. 7.461}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 41d}}; χαίταν **let fall**, {{Refs|Callias Epigrammaticus “Cer.”}}
**5** with _metaphorically_, κοινολογίας.. ἡδονὴν -χεούσης {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.14.}}
**3** _passive_, **to be poured over** the ground, **be there in heaps**, ὁ χῶρος, ἐν ᾧ αἱ ἄκανθαι [τῶν ὀφίων] κατακεχύαται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.75}}; of persons, **to be spread, dispersed**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.239 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**II** **cause to flow, run**, [χρυσὸν] ἐς πίθους τήξας κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.96:}} —_middle_, χρυσὸν καταχέασθαι **to have** it **melted down**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.50.}}

2710:
** καταχράομαι**,
Delph. καταχρέομαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 672.32 (2nd c.BC), +others}}: _future_ - χρήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 247b:}}_perfect_ -κέχρημαι both in _active_ and _passive_ senses (see.below): _aorist_ -εχρήσθην (see.below{{Refs|11.4)}}:—**make full use of, apply**, τινὶ εἰς.., ἐπί.., πρός τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 700c, “R.” 520a, “Cra.” 426e}}; μάρτυσι κ. πρὸς τὸ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 51a; κ. ἡ φύσις ἐν παρέργῳ τῇ.. ἀναπνοῇ πρὸς τὴν ὄσφρησιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Respiratione” 473a23, compare “Sens.” 444a25}}; λόγους.. οἷσπερ νυνὶ κατακέχρηται (in _active_ sense) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.44; ἐν καιρῷ [πράξεσι] κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.9; κενῇ προφάσει ταύτῃ κατεχρῶ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.150: with _accusative_, τὴν.. ὑπερβολὴν ἐπὶ βοήθειαν κ. ἡ φύσις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 663b33; σχολὴν ἐς ἀκρόασιν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Prom.” 4; τι εἴς τι “IG” 22.1672.307 (4th c.BC),compare 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 5.9.1: _absolutely_ in}}_perfect_ _participle_ in _passive_ sense, ἐξεύρημα.. Ἀθήνησιν.. κατακεχρημένον ἐν συμποσίοις ἰδίᾳ 'στί is **fashionable** at private banquets there, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 14.4.}}
**II** **do what one likes with** a person or thing, καταχρήσασθέ μοι, εἰ δοκῶ τοιοῦτος εἶναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.122.}}
**II.2** **use up, consume**, of money, etc., with _accusative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.22 (bis); στέαρ “PRev.Laws” 50.14 (3rd c.BC)}}; τὰ κρέα ἐν τὰν δαμοθοινίαν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 671 A 7 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; **lay out, apply** money, εἴς τι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.4, “IG” 9(1).694.34 (Corc., 2nd c.BC), 12(1).155.86 (from Rhodes)}}; ἐνταῦθα on this, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.50}}: _perfect_ in _active_ sense, ὅσα κατακέχρημαι Ἀθήνησι {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 5.69}}:—_passive_, **to be spent, consumed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.74; πλίνθου τῆς -χρησθείσης εἰς τοὺς τοίχους “PPetr.” 3p.139 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.3** **misuse, abuse**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.277: with _dative_, τῇ τῶν προγόνων δόξῃ μὴ -χρησόμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Mx.”}}[same place]; κ. ὀνόματι **use** it **in a wrong sense, misapply** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 270b24, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.43 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.1.2 (also _absolutely_, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.59 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; **fall into an error**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 279.11): with _accusative_, κ. τὴν σχολὴν εἰς τοῦτο Dionysius Epigrammaticus Comedy texts 4; τοῦ ἀρχαίου τι κ.}}**misappropriate,** {{Refs|Test.Epict. 8.8.}}
**II.4** of persons, in bad sense, **make away with, destroy, kill**, with _accusative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.82, 117, 4.146, +others, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.85.1:}} —_passive_, _aorist_ -χρησθῆναι, ἐδέοντό μιν κ. requested that he might **be put to death**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.120.}}
**III** **pretend, allege**, ὡς.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 43.39}}; ὅτι.. {{Refs|[prev. author] 48.44.}}
**B** _active_ **καταχράω** only _Ionic dialect_, used only in _3rd.pers._ _singular_, ἀντὶ λόφου ἡ λοφιὴ κατέχρα the mane **sufficed them** for a crest, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.70}}; elsewhere _impersonal_, **it suffices**, οὐδέ οἱ καταχρήσει.. ὑμέων ἀπέχεσθαι nor **will he be satisfied** to keep his hands off you, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.118}}; ὥς οἱ καταχρᾷ εἰ βούλονται that **it is sufficient** for him, if.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.164}}; καταχρήσει **it will suffice**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Phoenix Iambographus 2.21.}}

2711:
** κατα-ψύχω** [ῡ],
**cool, chill**, ὕδωρ κ. τὴν ξηρὰν ἀναθυμίασιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 361a2, compare 368b34; ὁ φόβος καταψύχει [prev. author] “PA” 650b28, compare “Pr.” 954b13, +others}}; αἱ ἄτομοι.. κατέψυξαν [τὸ σῶμα] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 60}}:—_passive_, _future_ - ψῠγήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 73.21}}: _perfect_ -έψυγμαι: _aorist_ -εψύχθην, also -εψύγην [ῠ] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 897a22}}:—**to be chilled, become cold**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.40, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 531b31, etc.}}; of persons, κατεψυγμένοι, opposed to θερμοί, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1389b30.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, οὐ -έψυξαν τὴν ὁρμήν **did** not **allow** their ardour **to cool**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.2.7}}:— _passive_, κατέψυκτο τὸ πρακτικόν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 46, compareCritodem. in “Cat.Codex Astr.” 8(1).259, Vett.Val. [prev. cited]}}
**3** **cool, refresh**, καταψύχει πνοή {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 127.}}
**II** **dry** land after irrigation, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 155 (3rd c.BC):}} — _passive_, of a country, χώρα κατεψυγμένη **dried** or **parched up**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.7, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 31.}}
**III** _intransitive_, **cool down**, of persons, {{LXX|LXX.Gen.18.4.}}

2716:
** κατεργ-άζομαι**,
_future_ -άσομαι, later _3rd.pers._ _singular_ -ᾶται {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 10.2 (2nd c.BC):}}_aorist_ κατειργασάμην, and (in _passive_ sense) κατειργάσθην (see. below): _perfect_ κατείργασμαι both in _active_ and _passive_ sense (see. below):— **effect by labour, achieve**, πρήγματα μεγάλα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.24; πᾶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1022; μόρον.. ἐπαλλήλοιν χεροῖν [prev. author] “Ant.” 57; ταῦθ᾽ ἁπινοεῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 247; τὰ δυνατά 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.65; τὰ πρὸς εὐδαιμονίαν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.31 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; μεγάλα μὲν ἐπινοεῖτε, ταχὺ δὲ κατεργάζεσθε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 2.2; κ. εἰρήνην τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.8}}; ἢν κατεργάσῃ if you **do the job**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 933}}: _perfect_ κατείργασμαι, μέγιστα ἔργα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.11}}: in _passive_ sense, **to have been effected** or **achieved**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.123, 141, 4.66, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1081, etc.; κατειργασμένη ὠφέλεια 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.4; ἐλθεῖν ἐπὶ κατειργασμένοις 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.9}}: _aorist_ -ειργάσθην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Herm.” 5.}}
**b** **earn, gain by labour, acquire**, τὴν ἡγεμονίην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.65; πόλει σωτηρίαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 1046; μεγάλα τῇ πόλει 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.229; τοῦτο 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.66; ὁ κατειργασμένος τὴν τυραννίδα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 473d: in _passive_ sense, ἀρετὴ ἀπὸ σοφίης κατεργασμένη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.102.}}
**c** _absolutely_, **achieve one's object, be successful**, αὐτὸς ἑωυτῷ {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.78}}; simply, **work**, ἐν τοῖς ἐργαστηρίοις {{Refs|“PTeb.unpublished” 703.148.}}
**2** with _accusative_ _person_, **make an end of, finish, kill**, ἑωυτόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 888, etc.; λέοντα βίᾳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1094.}}
**2.b** **overpower, subdue, conquer**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 842, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.85, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.59, etc.; τὴν Ἑλλάδα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.100}}; ποσὶ καὶ στόματι κ. [τινά] **attack** him, of a horse, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.111:}} in _passive_ sense, of land, μακέλλῃ τῇ κατείργασται πέδον **is subdued, brought under cultivation**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 526; κατεργαζομένη ἡ γῆ 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.1.3}}; later _transitive_, **cultivate,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 10.2 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**2.c** **prevail upon**, κατεργάσατο καὶ ἀνέπεισε Ξέρξην, ὥστε.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.16, 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 13.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 21; κ. τινὰ πειθοῖ 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.4.2:}} —_passive_, οὐκ ἐδύνατο κατεργασθῆναι [ἡ γυνή] could not **be prevailed upon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.108.}}
**2.d** with _double_ _accusative_, **do** something **to** one, καλόν τι τὴν πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 2.17 (but κ. τὴν πόλιν}} **carry on business in** the city, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“SIG” 899 (Mesambria, 3rd c.AD)).}}
**II** **till, cultivate** land, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 6.632.9 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; **work up for use**, frequently of food, by chewing or digestion, ὀδόντας ἔχει οἷς κ. τὴν τροφήν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 501b30, compare “Juv.” 469a31, “Spir.” 482b16, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.649 (_passive_)}}; τὸ -αζόμενον ἔχειν εὔρωστον a strong **digestion**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 17(2).430}}; κ. τὰ ἐδέσματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 714}}; by grinding (of corn), {{Refs 1st c.BC+|3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 3.30, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.13 (_passive_)}}; by ripening (of fruits), κατειργασμένα ἐπὶ τοῦ δένδρου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.367}}; κ. μέλι **make**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.194}}; κ. τὴν κόπρον **prepare** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 552a24; σίδηρον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.10; ξύλα-ειργασμένα 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.17.2; στίππυον τὸ κατειργαζμένον “PCair.Zen.” 472.9 (3rd c.BC)}}; λίθους {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.98.}}
**II.2** **turn** bullion **into coin,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 21.6 (3rd c.BC).}}
**III** **work at, practise**, ἄλλην μελέτην κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 88c.}}
**IV** κ. ὄρη **level** them, {{LXX+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 11.3.4 (from LXX.1Esd.4.4).}}

2718:
** κατέρχομαι**,
_future_ κατελεύσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.303, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.125, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 6.12.3 (but in good _Attic dialect_ κάτειμι, as also κατῄειν is always used for the _imperfect_):}}_aorist_ κατήλῠθον or κατῆλθον, _infinitive_ κατελθεῖ; _Doric dialect_ _subjunctive_ κατένθῃ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.165 (from Cyrene); 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}}_participle_ κατενθών, _perfect_ _participle_ κατηνθηκώς, see at {καθέρπω} {{Refs|11}}: _perfect_ κατελήλυθα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 675.24 (2nd c.BC)}}:—**go down**, Οὐλύμποιο κατήλθομεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.125, etc.; τιν᾽ ἀθανάτων ἐξ οὐρανοῦ ἀστερόεντος.. κατελθέμεν 6.109}}; **go down to the grave**, κ. Ἄϊδος εἴσω, Ἄϊδόσδε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 284, 7.330; εἰς Ἅιδου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1101, etc.: rarely with _accusative_, τίς.. σκότου πύλας ἔτλη κατελθεῖ; 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 149.2 (parody)}}; from high land to the coast, ἐπὶ νῆα θοὴν κατελεύσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.303; from country to town, 11.188}}; **down** the Nile, εἰς Ἀλεξάνδρειαν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PLille” 3.80 (3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**2** of things, κατερχομένης ὑπὸ πέτρης by the **descending** rock, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.484, 541}}; of a river, κατέρχεται ὁ Νεῖλος πληθύων **comes down** in flood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.19; κατελθόντος αἰφνιδίου τοῦ ῥεύματος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.75.}}
**3** κ. εἰς τὸν ἀγῶνα, Latin _descendere in certamen_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 7.324.}}
**4** with _accusative_, **come to** a place, ὑμέτερον δῶ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.115; ἀφθονία κατελήλυθε τὴν πόλιν 6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “Mag.” 3.76.}}
**5** of property, **pass** to, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PRein.” 42.28 (1st-2nd c.AD), “POxy.” 1704.5 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II** **come back, return**, especially **come back from exile**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.4, +others, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1647, “Ch.” 3, “Eu.” 462, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 601, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1165, 1167, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21a, “OGI” 90.20 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC)}}, etc.; φυγὰς κατελθών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 200; ὃς ἂν κατέλθῃ τήνδε γῆν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 39:}} in _passive_ sense, ὑπ᾽ ὀλιγαρχίας κατελθεῖν **to be brought back** by.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.68; compare κάτειμι 11, καθέρπω 11.}}

2719:
** κατεσθίω**,
_poetry_ and later **κατερῡκ-έσθω** {{NT+5th c.BC+|“APl.” 4.240 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus), NT.Mark.12.40, “Dialexeis 5th c.BC” 2.14}} (_passive_, 1.5, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PMag.Lond.” 46.279 (4th c.AD))}}: _future_ κατέδομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.89, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.363, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 588:}}_aorist_ κατέφαγον (see. καταφαγεῖν): _perfect_ κατεδήδοκα {{Refs|[prev. author] “V.” 838, “Pax” 386, etc.}}; _participle_ κατὰ.. ἐδηδώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.542}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ κατεδήδεσμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 11o e, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 161.3}}: _aorist_ κατηδέσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 35}}:—**eat up, devour**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} frequently of animals of prey, λέων κατὰ ταῦρον ἐδηδώς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.542}}; of a serpent, [νεοσσοὺς] κατήσθιε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.314, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.256}}; of a dolphin, κατεσθίει ὅν κε λάβῃσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.24}}; also of men, οἳ κατὰ βοῦς.. ἤσθιον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.8}}; τοὺς γονέας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.38, compare 8.115, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 341; [τυρὸν] αὐτοῖς τοῖς ταλάροις κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 560}}; κατεδηδόκασι τὰ λάχαν᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 15.12: with _genitive_ _partitive_, κ. πολλῶν πουλύπων 5th-6th c.BC: Amipsias Comicus 6.}}
**2** **eat up, devour** one's substance, τὰ κοινά, τὰ πατρῷα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 258, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 239; τὰ ὄντα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 38.27; πατρῴαν οὐσίαν 4th c.BC: Anaxippus Comicus 1.32.}}
**3** **corrode**, [ῥεύματα] κ. γνάθους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 19}}; λίθοι κατεδηδεσμένοι ὑπὸ σηπεδόνος Pl.[same place],{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare “Dialexeis 5th c.BC” 1.5 (_passive_)}}; of the wind, κ. τὰ ἄνθη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 2.7.5}}:—_passive_, **to be gnawed**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 5.17.7.}}
**4** **bite**, τοῦ παλαιστοῦ τὸ οὖς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.6.}}
**5** κ. ἑαυτόν, _metaphorically_, of remorse, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 29.32, “Ep.” 256.}}

2720:
** κατευθύν-ω**,
_poetry_ _imperfect_ κατευθύνεσκον {{Refs|“IGRom.” 4.507b (Pergam.)}}:—**make** or **keep straight**, τὴν πτῆσιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Incessu Animalium” 710a2; ναῦν τῷ πηδαλίῳ 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 13.18}}; βιοτῆς οἴακα κατευθύνεσκες ἐν οἴκῳ “IGRom.” [same place]:—_passive_, αἱ περιφοραὶ κατευθυνόμεναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 44b.}}
**2** **guide, direct**, τὰς φύσεις {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 809a}}; τινὰ εἰς τὸν αὑτοῦ δρόμον{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 847a; [τὸν ἐλέφαντα] τῷ δρεπάνῳ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 610a28}}; [ναῦν] {{Refs|[prev. author] “fragment” 11}}; κ. τὰς πράξεις ὁ θεός{{Refs 1st c.AD+|Aristeas 18; τὰ παρόντα πρὸς τὸ τέλος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 42; πρὸς τὰ βελτίονα τοὺς νέους [prev. author] 2.20d; τὴν ψυχήν [prev. work]780b; τὸν λόγον πρός τι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(2).362.}}
**3** κ. τινός **demand** an account **from** one, **condemn**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 945a, compare “IG” 22.1183.10 (probably), 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.22.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **make straight towards**, κατεύθυναν αἱ βόες ἐν τῇ ὁδῷ εἰς ὁδὸν Βαιθσάμυς {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.1Ki.6.12; κ. τῇ πτήσει ὄρθιον ἐπὶ τοὺς πολεμίους 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 33.}}
**II.2** **prosper**, {{LXX|LXX.Sir.29.18}}: with _genitive_, **succeed** in doing.., οὐ κατεύθυνε τοῦ λαλῆσαι οὕτως{{LXX|LXX.Judg.12.6.}}
**II.3** οἱ -ευθύνοντες the **righteous**,{{LXX|LXX.Pro.15.8.}}

2722:
** κατέχω**,
_future_ καθέξω (of duration) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.332, κατασχήσω (of momentary action) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.72, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.42:}}_aorist_ κατέσχον, _poetry_
**A** κατέσχεθον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 575, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 754}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ κάσχεθε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.702}}, _Aeolic dialect_ κατέσκ [εθε] {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” la.12; _imperative_ κατάσχες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 555 (Lyric poetry), later κατάσχε 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Epistulae” 38 (variant), “PMag.Lond.” 97.404}}; late _aorist_ κατέσχα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PGen.” 54.22 (4th c.AD).}}
**A.I** _transitive_, **hold fast**, καλύπτρην χείρεσσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 575.}}
**A.I.b** **hold back, withhold**, εἴ με βίῃ ἀέκοντα καθέξει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.186, compare 11.702, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.200; ἐν κολεῷ ξίφος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.6}}: **check, restrain, bridle**, ἑωυτόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.129, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 162c, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 112}}; [γυναῖκε] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 190; ἱππικὸν δρόμον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 754; δάκρυ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 204 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὀργήν, θυμόν, ὕβριν, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1011, “OC” 874, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 555 (Lyric poetry), etc.; δύνασιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 605 (Lyric poetry); τὴν διάνοιαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130}}; κ. τὴν ἀγωγήν **put** it **off**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.29}}; κ. τὸ πλῆθος ἐλευθέρως, ἰσχύϊ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.65, 3.62; κ. τινὰ πολέμῳ [prev. author] 1.103; τὰ δάκρυα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 117d, +others; τὸν γέλωτα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 184a, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 2.4}}; οὖρον **hold in**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.407 (but -όμενα [οὖρα] as a disease, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 1.59, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 16.639)}}; ἑαυτὸν κατέχει μὴ ἐπιπηδᾶν **restrains** himself **from**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254a}}:—_passive_, **to be held down**, γλῶσσα κατείχετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.50; ἐπιθυμίας -ομένας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 554c}}; **to be bound**, ὁρκίοισι μεγάλοισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.29; ὑποσχέσει “PAmh.” 2.97.17 (2nd c.AD)}}; τοῖς τινων ὀφειλήμασιν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 117.13 (3rd c.AD)}}; of a nation, **to be kept under** (by tyrants), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59.}}
**A.I.c** **detain**, κ. [αὐτοὺς] ἐνιαυτόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.128, compare 8.57, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.100; κ. [αὐτοὺς] ὥστε μὴ ἀπιέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.6.11}}:—_passive_, **to be detained, stay**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.117, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 249; περὶ Κρήτην 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.86, etc.}}
**A.I.d** in imprecations, **inhibit** (compare καταδέω{{Refs 4th c.BC+|(A) 111), “Tab.Defix.Aud.” 50.11 (4th c.BC), “PMag.Par.” 1.2077; Μανῆν καταδῶ καὶ κατέχω “Tab.Defix.” 109.}}
**A.I.e** **place under arrest,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PFlor. 61.60 (1st c.AD), etc.}}
**A.I.f** **keep** an oath, ὅρκον {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 526.39 (Itanos, 3rd c.BC).}}
**A.I.2** c._genitive_, **gain possession of, be master of**, τῶν ἐπιστημῶν μὴ πάνυ κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 9a6; τῆς ὀργῆς 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 185 codices 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes; τῆς παραποταμίας βίᾳ κατέσχον 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.82, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 14.1.9; τῆς Ἀσίας ἐθνῶν 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Praefatio” 9}}; **control**, τινων {{LXX|LXX.1Mac.6.27}}; ἑαυτῶν Erot.see entry προπετή; μηκέτι κατέχων ἑαυτοῦ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.15.1, compare 1.7.3}}; **cling to**, τῶν κεράτων τοῦ θυσιαστηρίου {{LXX|LXX.3Ki.1.51.}}
**A.II** **possess, occupy**, especiallyof rulers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 732 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 81 (_anapaest meter_)}}; σῴζειν ἅπερ ἃν ἅπαξ κατάσχωσι whatever they **have got**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.242}}; especially of property. **enjoy possession of,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 5.47 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc. (but also, **sequestrate,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PLille 3.16 (_passive_, 3rd c.BC), etc.)}}; ὡς μηδὲν ἔχοντες καὶ πάντα κατέχοντες {{NT|NT.2Cor.6.10.}}
**A.II.b** **dwell in, occupy**, Ὀλύμπου αἴγλαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 609 (Lyric poetry)}}; especially of tutelary gods, Παρνασίαν ὃς κ. πέτραν, of Dionysus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 603 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.1, “SIG” 662.10 (Delos, 2nd c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 10}}; of a place, μέσον ὀμφαλὸν γᾶς Φοίβου κ. δόμος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 223 (Lyric poetry)}}; of the dead. θήκας Ἰλιάδος γᾶς.. κατέχουσι **occupy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 454 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1167 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**A.II.2** of sound, **fill**, οἱ δ᾽ ἀλαλητῷ πᾶν πεδίον κατέχουσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.79}}; κ. στρατόπεδον δυσφημίαις **fill** it with his grievous cries, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 10; οἰμωγὴ.. κατεῖχε πελαγίαν ἅλα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 427, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1133 (Lyric poetry)}}:—_passive_, οἶκος κλαυθμῷ κατείχετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.111.}}
**A.II.3** πανδάκρυτον βιοτὰν κ. **continue to live** a life.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 690 (Lyric poetry).}}
**A.II.4** **to be spread over, cover**, νὺξ.. δνοφερὴ κάτεχ᾽ οὐρανόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.269; ἡμέρα πᾶσαν κατέσχε γαῖαν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 387, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 572 (Lyric poetry)}}; τίνες αὖ πόντον κατέχουσ᾽ αὖρα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 138; ὀσμὴ.. κατὰ πᾶν ἔχει δῶ 5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 82.9}}:—_passive_, σελήνη.. κατείχετο.. νεφέεσσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.145, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.368, 644}}:—_middle_, _Epic dialect_ _aorist_, κατέσχετο χερσὶ πρόσωπα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.361}}; κατασχομένη ἑανῷ **having covered her face**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.419.}}
**A.II.5** of the grave, **confine, cover**, τοὺς δ᾽ ἤδη κάτεχεν φυσίζοος αἶα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3.243, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.301, Oracle texts cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.67}}; as a threat, πάρος τινὰ γαῖα καθέξει sooner shall earth **cover** many a one, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.629, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.427, etc.}}
**A.II.6** of circumstances, etc., **hold fast, have** one **in their power**, μιν κατὰ γῆρας ἔχει χεῖράς τε πόδας τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.497}}; ὃν θάνατος δακρυόεις καθέχει (sic) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.987; ἐχθρὰ Φάλαριν κ. φάτις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.96; τινὰ.. λάθα κ. [prev. author] “N.” 8.24; [φθορὰ] κ. τὸν σὸν δόμον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 370}}; τύχη, πόλεμος κ. τινά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 304c, “Ep.” 317a}}; κ. κίνδυνος Σικελίαν{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 355d; συνέβη λοιμώδη νόσον κατασχεῖν τὴν Ἰταλίαν 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.12.1}}:—_passive_, ὑπὸ μεγάλης ἀνάγκης κατεχόμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 858a}}: rarely in good sense, ὁ δ᾽ ὄλβιος, ὃν φᾶμαι κατέχοντ᾽ ἀγαθαί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.10; μεγάλαι κ. τύχαι γένος ὀρνίθων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1726 (Lyric poetry); εὐμοιρίας -εχούσης τὸν βίον 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.5.1.}}
**A.II.6.b** of circumstances, etc., **prevail, prevail among, engage**, ἄλλα τῶν κατεχόντων πρηγμάτων χαλεπώτερα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.40, compare 1.65}}; μεγάλοι θόρυβοι κατέχουσ᾽ ἡμᾶς murmurs **are rife among** us, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 142 (_anapaest meter_)}}; φήμης ἀθρόας -σχούσης τὸ Ἑλληνικόν a sudden rumour **having overspread** Greece, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 8.15.}}
**A.II.7** **seize, occupy**, in right of **conquest**, τὸ Καδμείων πέδον uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 381}}; especially in historical writers, -σχήσειν [τὴν ἀκρόπολιν] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.72; τὰ πρήγματα [prev. author] 3.143; τὰ ἐχυρά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.27; τὰ κύκλῳ τῆς Ἀττικῆς ἁρμοσταῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.96; φρουραῖς τὰς πόλεις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.177d.}}
**A.II.8** **achieve, effect** an object, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.25; πρᾶξιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1312a33.}}
**A.II.9** **master, understand**, οὐ κατέχω τί βούλει φράζειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 26c, compare “Men.” 72d, 1st c.AD: Cebes Philosophus 34; περὶ φύσεως κ. πάντας τοὺς λόγους 3rd c.BC(?): Sosipater Comicus 1.17, compare 33}}; κ. νοῦν στίχων **grasp** the sense of.., {{Refs|Puchstein “epigram Gr.” p.9.}}
**A.II.9.b** **keep in mind, remember**, χρήσιμον καὶ τοῦτο κατασχεῖν τὸ στοιχεῖον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.10U., compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 26.2, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 109}}; κ. τινὰ ὀψοφάγον Chrysipp.Tyan. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.5e}}; κ. ὅτι, διότι, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 60.10 (3rd c.BC), 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.15}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.31U.}}
**A.II.10** **possess**, of a god, εἰ θεός ἐστιν ὁ σὰς κατέχων φρένας {{Refs|“PLit.Lond.” 52.12}}; τοιοῦτος ἔρως κατεῖχε τὴν ἄνθρωπον she **was** so **infatuated**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 23}}; of an actor, κ. τὸ θέατρον **held** the audience **spellbound**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 29}} (but, **kept** the audience **waiting, Phoc.**{{Refs|19)}}; of poets, μύθοις [τοὺς ἀκούοντας] κ. [{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 39 (variant{κατηχοῦσι})}}]:—mostly in _passive_, of persons, **to be possessed, inspired**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 533e}}; ἐξ Ὁμήρου{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 536b; ἐκ θεῶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.10; κάρῳ 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.18}}; τὸ θέατρον κατείχετο the audience **was spellbound**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.247 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; of hydrophobia patients, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 4.11}}; of a lover, τῷ αὐτῷ θεῷ (i.e. Ἔρωτι) κατέσχημαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 19.1}}:—also in _aorist_ _middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 244e.}}
**A.III** **follow close upon, press hard**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.22 (uncertain reading), “Cyn.” 6.22:}} —_passive_ {{Refs|[prev. work] 9.20.}}
**A.IV** **bring** a ship **to land**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.101, 7.59, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.162a.}}
**B** _intransitive_,
**B.1** (i.e. ἑαυτόν) **control oneself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 782; οὐκέτι καθέξω 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 394; εἶπεν οὖν μὴ κατασχών 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 15; οὐ κατέσχεν 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 3.43}}: with _infinitive_, κ. τὸ μὴ δακρύειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 117c.}}
**B.1.b** **stop, cease**, of the wind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 944 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.2** **come from the high sea to shore, put in** (see. supr. IV), νηΐ Θορικόνδε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Cererem” 126; τῆς Μαγνησίης χώρης ἐς τὸν αἰγιαλόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.188, compare 6.101, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.25.7, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 21}}; τίνες ποτ᾽ ἐς γῆν τήνδε.. κατέσχετ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 221, compare 270, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 83 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.21, etc.: with _accusative_ loci, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1206, “Cyc.” 223}}; of a journey by land, **rest**, προξένων δ᾽ ἔν του κατέσχε; {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ion” 551, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.71.2: _metaphorically_, εὖ κατασχήσει}} **shall come** safe **to land**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 503 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.3** **prevail**, ὁ λόγος κ. the report **prevails**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10; κληδὼν ἐν ἁπάσῃ τῇ πόλει κατεῖχεν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.130; σεισμῶν -εχόντων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.89; ὁ βορέας κατεῖχεν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 345a1, compare 360b33, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 1.5.1.}}
**B.4** **gain the upper hand**, παρά τινι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 262}}; **gain one's purpose**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.42; ὁ δὲ κατεῖχε τῇ βοῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 434; νομίζοντες ῥᾳδίως κατασχήσειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1307b10.}}
**C** _middle_, **keep back for oneself, embezzle**, [τὰ χρήματα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.164.}}
**C.2** **cover oneself**, see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.4.}}
**C.3** **hold, contain**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.26a. 7.}}
**C.II** _aorist_ _middle_, ={κατέχω} {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 2, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.284.}}
**C.II.2** in _passive_ sense, τεαῖς ῥιπαῖσι κατασχόμενος **subdued**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.10}}; καρδίαν κατέσχετο ἔρωτι **was seized with, possessed by**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 27}}; see above{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.10.}}

2723:
** κατηγορ-έω**,
**speak against**, especially before judges, **accuse**, opposed to ἀπολογέομαι, with _genitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.113, 8.60, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.21, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1073, etc.; τῆς πόλεως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 244e: less frequently κατά τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.9}}; κ. τινὸς πρὸς τὴν πόλιν **denounce** him publicly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 2c}}; κατηγόρεις [αὐτῶν] ὡς λέγοιεν you **accused** them of saying, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.134, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.38}}; κ. τινὸς ὅτι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|.. [prev. work] 1.7.17; τῶν ἱππέων ἐλθὼν πρὸς ὑμᾶς εἰς τὴν ἐκκλησίαν κατηγόρει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.197; τῆς ἐμῆς [τύχης] κατηγορεῖς [prev. author] 18.266, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.4.}}
**2** κ. τί τινος **bring as a charge against** a person, **accuse** him **of** it, δείν᾽ ἔπη μου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 514, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 28, etc.; τῶν ἄλλων μωρίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.4; ὃς ἐμοῦ Φιλιππισμὸν κατηγορεῖ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.294; κ. τι κατά τινος 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 23; τινὸς περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.110, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.85}}: with _infinitive_, κ. τινὸς παθεῖν τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 482c: with double _genitive_, παρανόμων κ. τινός 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.5.}}
**3** with _accusative_ _of things_ only, **allege in accusation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.113; μωρίαν ἐμήν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 418; τὴν πονηρίαν τῶν γονέων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 346a; τὰ γεγονότα κ. 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.10, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 932, “Ra.” 996 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.31, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.9}}:—_passive_, **to be brought as an accusation against**, κατηγορεῖτο τοὐπίκλημα τοῦτό μο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 529; ἀδικία πολλὴ κ. αὐτοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.95}}; τὰ πρῶτά μου ψευδῆ -ημένα the first false **charges brought against** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18a, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.9; τὰ -ηθέντα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.85, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.24; τἀδικήμαθ᾽ ἃ κατηγορεῖται 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.136: _impersonal_, followed by}}_infinitive_, σφέων.. κατηγόρητο μηδίζειν **a charge had been brought against** them that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.205; κατηγορεῖτό τινος ὡς βαρβαρίζοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.35}}; κατηγορουμένου δ᾽ αὐτοῦ, ὅτι.. **a charge being brought against** him, that.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 3.5.25.}}
**3.b** rarely in _passive_, of the person, **to be accused**, οἱ κατηγορούμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.7, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 38 (assuming variant).}}
**4** _absolutely_, **to be an accuser, appear as prosecutor**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 840, 842, “Pl.” 917, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 18e, etc.}}
**II** **signify, indicate, prove**, c._accusative_ _of things_, [τὸ νεαρὸν] κ. τὴν ὀλιγοετίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.3, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.695d, 4th c.AD: Adamantius Physiognomonicus 1.5, +others; ἀσθένειαν μᾶλλον ἢ δύναμιν 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.6.3}}; **display**, οἱ πολλὴν -οῦντες ἀπειροκαλίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 21: with _genitive_ _person_, εὖ γὰρ φρονοῦντος ὄμμα σοῦ κατηγορεῖ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 271, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 690, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 907, etc.; ὠκύτητα κ. τοῦ κυνός 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.26.}}
**II.2** followed by _relative_, **declare, assert**, αὐτὸ κατηγορέει τὸ οὔνομα ὡς ἔστι Ἑλληνικόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.115}}; κ. ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.189, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 73b (_impersonal_)}}: _absolutely_, **make a definite assertion**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 208b.}}
**III** in Logic, **predicate of** a person or thing, τί τινος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 3a19, +others, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 250}}; κυρίως, καταχρηστικῶς κ., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 5.15; ἐναντίως ὑπὲρ τῶν αὐτῶν [prev. author] “Oec.” p.60 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}: —more frequently in _passive_, **to be predicated of**.., τινος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 2a21, “APr.” 26b9, +others; κατά τινος [prev. author] “Cat.” 2a37; κατὰ παντὸς ἢ μηδενός [prev. author] “APr.” 24a15: less frequently ἐπί τινος [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 998b16, 999a15; so later ἐφ᾽ ἑνὸς οἴονται θεοῦ ἑκάτερον τῶν ὀνομάτων -εῖσθαι 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.48; περί τινος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 140b37; τὸ κοινῇ -ούμενον ἐπὶ πᾶσιν [prev. author] “SE” 179a8: _absolutely_, τὸ κατηγορούμενον}} **the predicate**, opposed to τὸ ὑποκείμενον (the subject), {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cat.” 1b11, compare “Metaphorical” 1043a6, +others}}; κατηγορεῖν καὶ -εῖσθαι **to be subject** and **predicate**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “APr.” 47b1.}}
**III.2** **affirm**, opposed to ἀπαρνέομαι, {{Refs|[prev. work] 41a10.}} _adverb_ κατηγορουμένως **categorically, roundly**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.90 W.}}

2724:
** κατηγορ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, **accusation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.50, etc.}}; opposed to αἰτία (expostulation), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.69; opposed to ἔπαινος, [prev. work] 84}}; opposed to ἀπολογία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1358b11; τὴν κ. ποιεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.6; ὡς ὑβρίζοντος κ. ἐποιοῦντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.1}}; κ. ἐγένοντο πολλαὶ τῶν Ἀθηναίων **charges** were made against.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HG” 2.1.31; κατηγορίαι κατά τινος γεγόνασιν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.147}}; εἰ.. ἐπὶ τοῖς πεπραγμένοις κατηγορίας ἔχω I am liable to **accusation**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.240.}}
**II** in Logic, **predication**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1007a35, etc.: _plural_, [prev. author] “APo.” 84a1}}; especially **affirmative predication**, opposed to στέρησις, {{Refs|[prev. author] “APr.” 52a15; ἄπορον ἐν κ. “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 2.93.}}
**II.2** **predicate**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1004a29, 1028a28, +others, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.23U., etc.}}
**II.3** more frequently, **category, head of predicables**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 103b20 (ten), “APo.” 83b16, “Ph.” 225b5 (eight), “Metaphorical” 1068a8 (seven), compare “EN” 1096a29.}}

2727:
** κατηχ-έω**,
**sound over** or **through**, ἁρμονία κ. τῆς θαλάττης {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.19, compare 2.12.}}
**2** **sound amiss**, opposed to συνηχέω, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Vitruvius Scriptor de Architectura Latinus 5.8.1.}}
**II** **teach by word of mouth**: hence generally, **instruct**, κ. τινὰ πολλὰ τῶν ἀγνοουμένων Agrippa 11 cited in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Vita” 65, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 48, “PStrassb.” 41.37 (3rd c.AD)}}:—_passive_, **to be informed** or **instructed**, περί τινος {{NT|NT.Act.21.21}}; κ. ὅτι.. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.575; ὥσπερ κατηχήμεθα 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 5.5.}}
**II.2** in **NT, instruct in the elements of religion**, {{NT|NT.1Cor.14.19}}:—_passive_, περὶ ὧν κατηχήθης λόγων {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Luke.1.4; ὁ κατηχούμενος τὸν λόγον NT.Gal.6.6; κατηχημένος τὴν ὁδὸν τοῦ Κυρίου NT.Act.18.25, compare 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “adversus Christianos - Abh. Berl. Akad.” 26.}}

2729:
** κατισχύω**,
_future_ -ύσω {{NT|NT.Matt.16.18}}:— **overpower, prevail over**, τινα {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 74, Aristeas 21, LXX.2Ch.8.3, +others; ὅταν ἡ τῆς πείρας ἀκρίβεια -ισχύῃ τὴν τῶν λόγων πιθανότητα 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.39: also with _genitive_, LXX.Jer.15.18, 3rd c.AD: Alexander Aphrodisiensis Philosophus “in Aristotelis Topicorum libros octo commentaria - pars” 248.19}}; [τῆς ἐκκλησίας] {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Matt.16.18; τινὸς σοφίᾳ 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 5.19; Ἄρης κ. τῆς Σελήνης Vett. Val. 104.10}}; γενναίας φύσεως {{Refs|Chor.in “Rev.Phil.” 1.57}}:—_passive_, **to be worsted**, ὑπ᾽ ἔρωτος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.71; τῇ μάχῃ [prev. author] 17.45.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **have the upper hand, prevail**, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.17.11, +others}}; κ. τῷ πλήθει **to be superior in**.., {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.13.3; κατίσχυον αἱ φωναὶ αὐτῶν NT.Luke.23.23.}}
**2.b** **to be prevalent**, ὁρμαὶ καὶ ζῆλοι παρά τισι κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.4.6; κατισχυούσης τῆς θερμότητος 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.11.7; κατίσχυκεν ἡ φήμη παρὰ τοῖς πλείστοις 3rd c.BC: Antigonus Carystius Paradoxographus “Mirabilia” 152.}}
**II** **come to one's full strength**, δέμας in body, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 346, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.189 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**III** _transitive_, **strengthen, encourage**, with _accusative_, {{LXX|LXX.Deu.1.38, +others}}; τὰς χεῖράς τινων{{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.1Esd.7.15; οὐδετέραν τῶν στάσεων 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.65.}}

2730:
** κατοικ-έω**,
**settle in, colonize**, πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.164; γῆν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 10}}; τοῖς κατοικεῖν ἐθέλουσι τὰν πόλιν Decrees Byzantine cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.91}}: generally, **inhabit**, τόπους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 40; τὴν Ἀσίαν “SIG” 557.17 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., 3rd c.BC)}}, etc.:— _passive_, **to be dwelt in** or **inhabited**, opposed to κατοικίζομαι (to be just founded), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1266b2.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **settle, dwell**, ζητοῦσα.. ποῦ κατοικοίης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 362; ἵνα χρὴ κατοικεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 153}}; ἐν δόμοις, ἐν ἄστεσι, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1651, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 666e, etc.; αὐτόθι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.34; ἐν μοναρχίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.36; ἐπὶ γῆς NT.Rev.3.10}}; especially of non-citizens, Ἐφέσιοι καὶ οἱ -οῦντες {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 352.4 (Ephesus, 4th c.BC). compare 633.67 (Milet., 2nd c.BC)}}:—_perfect_ and _pluperfect_ _passive_, **to have been planted** or **settled**, κατὰ κώμας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.96, compare 2.102}}; κ.νῆσον, τὴν μεσόγειαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.8, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120.}}
**II** **administer, govern**, οἱ τὰς πόλεις -οῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.225 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus:}} —more frequently in _passive_, κατῴκηνται καλῶς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|of Athens, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1004}}; ὀρθῶς κ., of Sparta, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 683a.}}
**III** _intransitive_ of cities, **lie, be siluated**, ἐν τοῖς πεδίοις {{Refs|[prev. work] 677c, compare 682c: also with _accusative_ loci, τὰς τὴν Ἀσίαν κατοικούσας}}**which are situated in**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.123.}}

2731:
** κατοίκ-ησις**, εως, ἡ,
**settling in** a place, διὰ τὴν ταύτῃ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.15.}}
**II** **dwelling, abode**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 71b, “Criti.” 115c, LXX.Gen.10.30, etc.; τὴν κ. εἶχεν ἐν τοῖς μνήμασι NT.Mark.5.3}}; **inhabited district**, ἡ κατὰ τὴν Ἰταλίαν κ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.523e.}}

2733:
** κατοικ-ία**, ἡ,
**habitation**, βαρβάρων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus 119 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus; τόπος εὐφυὴς πρὸς κ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.78.5; ὑγιεινὴν ποιεῖν τὴν κ. 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.4.8}}; **farm, village**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.32.4, etc.}}: generally, **dwelling-place,** {{NT|NT.Acts.17.26}}; **domicile**, Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Chr.” 31 i 23 (2nd c.BC).}}
**2** **settlement, colony**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.4.11}}; especially of **military colonies** in Egypt, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 61 (b). 227 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc.; also, = Latin _colonia_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 6.2.5, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 16, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.19}}; κατοικίαι πόλεων foundation of **colonies**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 47.}}
**3** **body of residents in** a foreign **city**, ἡ κ. τῶν ἐν Ἱεραπόλει κατοικούντων Ἰουδαίων {{Refs|“IGRom.” 4.834.}}

2734:
** κατοπτρ-ίζω**,
**show as in a mirror** or **by reflexion**, τοῦ-ίζοντος [τὴν ἶριν] ἀστέρος {{Refs|“Placita Philosophorum” 3.5.11}}:—_passive_, **to be mirrored**, {{Refs|Anonymus “Oxy.” 1609.19.}}
**II** _middle_, **look into a mirror, behold oneself in it**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.66, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.48, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 15.687c, etc.}}
**II.2** **behold as in a mirror**, ἰδέαν {{NT+1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.107; δόξαν Κυρίου NT.2Cor.3.18}} (but here perhaps **reflect**).

2735:
** κατόρθ-ωμα**, ατος, τό,
**success**, opposed to εὐτύχημα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Magna Moralia” 1199a13, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.19.12, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.54, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.22, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 10}}; of literary style, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 33.1, 36.2: _plural_, opposed to ἀποτεύγματα, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.35 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}; variant for διορθ-, {{NT|NT.Act.24.2 (_plural_).}}
**2** **that which is done rightly, virtuous action**, in _plural_, opposed to ἁμαρτήματα, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.295, +others, compare “IG” 5(2).268.15 (Mantinea, 1st c.BC)}}, etc.; τῶν καθηκόντων τὰ τέλεια, ={τὰ κ}., {{Refs|“Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.134.}}
**3** **perfection**, τέλος καὶ πέρας καὶ κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.173 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, compare 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 9.16.}}
**4** Grammars, **correct use**, opposed to βαρβαρισμός, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.124.}}

2736:
** κάτω**,
_adverb_, (κατά):
**I** with Verbs implying Motion, **downwards**, ἐπισκύνιον κ. ἕλκεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.136; κ. ὁρόων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.91; κατὰ τείχεος κ. ῥίπτειν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.53; κατώρυξέν με κατὰ τῆς γῆς κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 238; χώρει κ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 74; κ. δάκρυ᾽ εἰβομένη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 527 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 384}}; especially of the nether world, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 839, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 197, etc.}}; κ. βλέπειν, φέρεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 500b, 584e}}; κ. διεχώρει αὐτοῖς they suffered from diarrhoea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.20, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.20}}; φάρμακον πῖσαι κ. give a purgative, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aff.” 32, compare 15}}; κ. βοηθεῖν go **down** to help, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 32.5}}; for ἄνω καὶ κάτω, ἄνω κάτω, etc., see at {ἄνω} (B) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.2.}}
**I.2** **downwards**, in a chain of causes, ἐπὶ τὸ κ. ἰέναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 994a19.}}
**I.3** with _genitive_, **down from**, πετρῶν ὦσαι κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 448}}.
**II** with Verbs implying Rest (so more frequently in Prose), **beneath, below**, opposed to ἄνω, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 301, etc.: ὁ τόπος ὁ κ. καλούμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 112c.}}
**II.b** **in the world below**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 660, “OC” 1563 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.; οἱ κ. **the dead**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 865, “Ant.” 75, etc.; οἱ κ. θεοί [prev. author] “El.” 292, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 851.}}
**II.c** geographically **below, southward**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, see at {ἄνω} (B) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.1e}}]; also κ. οἰκεῖν to dwell **on the coast**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.7; οἱ κ., opposed to οἱ τὴν μεσόγειαν κατῳκημένοι, [prev. work] 120}}; ἡ. κ. Γαλατία **lower** Galatia, Plu {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Aem.” 9, etc.; βασιλεὺς τῶν τε ἄνω καὶ τῶν κ. χωρῶν “OGI” 90.3 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC).}}
**II.d** in the race-course, τὰ κ. **the starting-place**, opposed to τὰ ἄνω (the goal), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 613b.}}
**II.e** τὰ κ. τῶν μελῶν **the lower** limbs of the body, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 794d}}; ἡ κ. κοιλία, opposed to ἡ ἄνω, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 360b24. “PA” 676a5}}; περὶ τὰ κ. χωρεῖν **miscarry, fail**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 1.}}
**II.f** of Time, **afterwards, later**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 5.13; οἱ κ. χρόνοι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Coriolanus” 25}}; οἱ κ., opposed to οἱ πάλαι, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hipp.” 1}}; τοῦ χρόνου κ. **later** in time, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 3.17, “NA” 2.18; Δαρεῖος ὁ κ. [prev. work] 6.48}}; compare ἄνω (B) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.1i.}}
**II.g** in Logic, τὰ κ. **the lower members** in a descending series of genera and species, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Anthologia Graeca” 0.97a31, “Metaphorical” 992a18.}}
**III** with _genitive_, **under, below**, κ. χθονός, γῆς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 1023, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 968, etc.}}
**IV** _comparative_ κατωτέρω **lower, downwards**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 70, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 173.2}}: with _genitive_, **lower than, below**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.132.}}
**IV.2** _superlative_ κατωτάτω **at the lowest part**, τὰ κ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.125 (but in signification 11.g, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 29).}}

2737:
** κατώτερος**, α, ον,
_comparative_ _adjective_ from κάτω, **lower**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 31, LXX.3Ki.9.17, etc.; τὰ κ. μέρη τῆς NT.Eph.4.9}}; **more southerly**, {{Refs|Vett. Val. 34.21.}}
**2** of Time, **later, younger**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Cererem” 131.}} _adverb_ κατωτέρω, see at {κάτω}.

2738:
** καῦμα**, ατος, τό,
(καίω) **burning heat**, especially of the sun, καύματος ἔξ after **sun-heat**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.865, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 415, 588, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 39, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 417, Epinic. 1.10, etc.}}; πρὶν ἂν τὸ κ. παρέλθῃ the **heat of the day**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 242a, compare “Ti.” 70d; ἐὰν ᾖ κ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 342b10: frequently in _plural_, ἡλίου τε καύμασιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 350, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.104, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.9, etc.; [τόποι] ὑπὸ καυμάτων διαφθειρόμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.12; καύματα καὶ χειμῶνες 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 87}}: in _plural_, also of frost, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.98b, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Lex.” 2.}}
**2** **fever heat**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49}}; of **inflamed** conditions, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 19, “Aph.” 7.13: _metaphorically_, of love, κ. ἀρσενικόν “Anthologia Graeca” 12.87.}}
**II** in _plural_, **holes burnt by cautery**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 11, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 863a31.}}
**III** **brand** on cattle, {{Refs|“IG” 7.3171.44 (Orchom. _Boeotian dialect_).}}
**IV** **embers** of sacrifices, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 12o d.}}
**V** **firewood,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|PLond. 3.1166.6, +others (1st c.AD).}}

2740:
** καῦσις**, εως, ἡ,
**burning**, [τῶν ἱρῶν] {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.40; λύχνοι τῆς κ. LXX.Exo.39.17 (37), compare “PLond.” 3.1177.74 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** in Surgery, **cautery**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Mochl.” 3; ἢ καύσει ἢ τομῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 406d: in _plural_, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 426b, “Ti.” 65b.}}
**III** in _plural_, **burning heat**, ψύξεις τε καὶ κ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 156b.}}
**IV** **smelting**, χαλκοῦ, ἀργύρου, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.6.5.}}

2741:
** καυσ-όω**,
**heat**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 18}}:—_passive_, **burn with intense heat**, {{NT|NT.2Pet.3.10, 12}}: generally, **to be burnt,** {{Refs|PHolm. 25.27.}}
**II** **suffer from** καῦσος (A) 1, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 9.13.1, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.720; καυσουμένη ἐπιφάνεια 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.134.}}

2742:
** καύσ-ων**, ωνος, ὁ,
**burning heat, summer heat,** {{NT+3rd c.BC+|NT.Matt.20.12, 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 264, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philops.” 25; καύσωνος ὥρᾳ 3rd c.BC: Diphilus Siphnius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.73a}}; ἄνεμος καύσων **sirocco**, {{LXX|LXX.Jer.18.17, +others}}; κ. alone,{{LXX+NT+1st c.AD+|LXX.Judg.8.3, NT.Jam.1.11, 1st c.AD: Athenaeus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 1.2.13, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 85.}}
**2** κ. στομάχου **heartburn**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.22.}}
**3** κ. πυρετός, ={καῦσος} (A) 1, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Alexander Trallianus Medicus “de febribus” 2.}}
**II** ={διψάς}{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|11, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 6.51, 3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 20.1.}}

2745:
** καύχ-ημα**,
_Doric dialect_ **καύχ-ᾱμα**, ατος, τό, **a boast, vaunt**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).51.}}
**2** **subject of boasting**, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lesbonax Rhetor 3.4 (_plural_), NT.Rom.4.2.}}

2748:
** κέδρος**, ἡ,
**cedar-tree**, ὀδμὴ κέδρου.. θύου τ᾽ ἀνὰ νῆσον ὀδώδει δαιομένων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.60; τὸ ἀπὸ κ. ἄλειφαρ γινόμενον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.87, compare 4.75}}; applied to **prickly cedar, Juniperus Oxycedrus**, Od.[same place], {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.12.3}}; **Syrian cedar, J. excelsa**,{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 3.2.6, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.77}}; **Phoenician cedar, J. phoenicea**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.2.3}}; **Himalayan cedar, J. macropoda**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.29}}; κ. μικρά **juniper, J. communis**, Dsc. [prev. cited]
**2** **anything made of cedar- wood: cedar coffin**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 365 (_plural_), “Tr.” 1141}}; **cedar box**, for a bee- hive, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.81.}}
**3** **cedar-oil**, τῇ κ. ἀλείφειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ind.” 16.}}
**4** see at {κέδρον} {{Refs|1.}}

2749:
** κεῖμαι**, κεῖσαι
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.319, etc. (κατά-κειαι “hymnus ad Mercurium” 254, 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus κεῖοι “Tab.Defix.” in “Philol.” 59.201), κεῖται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.47, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9, 4.62 (variant κέεται), “IG” 12.94.25; _plural_ κεῖνται 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 242}}, _Ionic dialect_ κέᾰται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.659, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (προσ-κέανται is _falsa lectio_ 1.133, compare προσ-κέαται, variant -κέονται, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 6), κείᾰται 6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 11.6 (κατα- 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.567), κέονται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.510, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.232, probably in 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 94, συγ-κέονται 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 2.4}}; _imperative_ κεῖσο, κείσθω, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.178, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.171}}; _subjunctive_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ κέηται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 257c, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 113, _Epic dialect_ κεῖται (from κέψ-ε-ται) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.32, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.102, +others, δια-κέησθε 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.259, κείωνται “IG” 22.1176.21}}; _optative_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ κέοιτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.67, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 14 (κατα-), 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.32, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 477a}}; _infinitive_ κεῖσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.126, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 3, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.127, +others, κέεσθαι variant in [prev. work] 2, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 6, 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἰσορροπιῶν” 1 “Prooem.”}}; _participle_ κεί μενος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.265, etc.}}: _imperfect_ ἐκείμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.284, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ κείμην {{Refs|9.434}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ κέσκετο {{Refs|21.41, (παρε-) 14.521}}; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐκέατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.167}}, _Epic dialect_ κέατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.763, κείατο 11.162; κεῖντο 21.426, (ἐπέ-) 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.19}}: _future_ κείσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.121, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 895, etc.}}, _Doric dialect_ κεισεῦμαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.53}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _śéte_ (={κεῖται}), also **śáyate** 'lie', Gr. κοίτη, κοιμάομαι, perhaps Latin _cunae_, etc.):—**to be laid** (used as _passive_ to τίθημι): hence, **lie, lie outstretched**, used by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} mostly with Preps., πυρὴν.. ᾗ ἔνι κεῖται Πάτροκλος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.210; κεῖτο παρὰ μνηστῇ ἀλόχῳ 9.556; ἐπὶ γαίῃ 11.162; ὑπ᾽ αἰθούσῃ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.390; also ἐπί τινος, ὀστέα.. κείμεν᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἠπείρου 1.162; τὸ δ᾽ ἥμισυ κεῖτ᾽ ἐπὶ γαίης 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.565, compare 20.345}}; but ὁ δ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ἐννέα κεῖτο πέλεθρα **lay stretched** over.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.577, +others}}; later κεῖσθαι εἰς.., in pregnant sense, εἰς ἀνάγκην κείμεθ᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 620; εἰς ὀλίγην κ. κόνιν “Anthologia Graeca” 9.677 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus)}}; also ἐπὶ τὴν ὁδὸν κ. **to be strewn** upon the path, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Iambi” 1.250: Architecture texts, κείμενον σχῆμα, opposed to ὠρθωμένον}}, **plan**, opposed to elevation, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus “Abhandlungen der Göttinger Gesellschaft der Wissenschaften (Phil.-hist. Klasse)” 163.3: c._accusative_, τόπον.. ὅντινα κεῖται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 145 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**2** **lie down to rest, repose**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.281, etc.; πορφυρέᾳ κείμενος ἐν χλανίδι 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 37.12}}; **lie, remain**, κεῖτο γὰρ ἐν νήεσσι.. Ἀχιλλεύς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.688, compare 7.230, etc.; οὐ χρῆν ἥσυχον κεῖσθαι πόδα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 142.13}}; **lie still**, λασίην ὑπὸ γαστέρ᾽ ἐλυσθεὶς κείμην, of Odysseus under the ram's belly, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.434: _metaphorically_, κακὸν κείμενον}} a **sleeping** evil, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 510 (Lyric poetry); τοῦ κύματος κειμένου 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 15.5.}}
**3** **lie sick** or **wounded**, ἐν νήσῳ κεῖτο, of Philoctetes, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.721, compare 15.240; κείσεται οὐτηθείς 8.537, compare 11.659; γήραϊ λυγρῷ κεῖται ἐνὶ μεγάροις ἀρημένος 18.435; κεῖτ᾽ ὀλιγηπελέων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.457}}; **lie in misery**, ἐοικότι κεῖται ὀλέθρῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1.46; κεῖται ἐν ἄλγεσι θυμός 21.88, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 183 (Lyric poetry); κ. ἐν κακοῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1639, “Hec.” 969}}; κειμένῳ ἐπεμπηδᾶν to kick him **when he's down**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 550.}}
**4** **lie dead**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.467, 16.541, +others, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1438, 1446, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 359; κεῖται δὲ νεκρὸς περὶ νεκρῷ [prev. author] “Ant.” 1240}}: rare in Prose, χίλιοι.. νεκροὶ κείμενοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.25, compare 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.1.8.}}
**4.b** frequently also in epitaphs, **lie buried**, τῇδε κείμεθα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 92, compare 97; κεῖσαι ζῶν ἔτι μᾶλλον τῶν ὑπὸ γᾶς [prev. author] 60; also κ. ἐν Ταρ τάρῳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.15}}; ἐν τάφῳ, ἐν Ἅιδου, παρ᾽ Ἅιδῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 895, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 463, “OT” 972}}; also in Prose, τὸν χῶρον ἐν τῷ κέοιτο Ὀρέστης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.67, compare 4.11, 9.105, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.43}}; κ.ὑπό τινων **to be buried** by.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.583c.}}
**5** frequently of a corpse, **lie unburied**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.338, 19.32; κεῖται.. νέκυς ἄκλαυτος ἄθαπτος 22.386; μὴ δή με ἕλωρ Δαναοῖσιν ἐάσῃς κεῖσθαι 5.685}}; also κεῖτ᾽ ἀπόθεστος.. ἐν πολλῇ κόπρῳ **lay uncared for**, of the old hound of Odysseus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.296; εὐνὴ.. κάκ᾽ ἀράχνια κεῖται ἔχουσα 16.35}}; of places, **lie in ruins**, δόμοι.. χαμαιπετεῖς ἔκεισθ᾽ ἀεί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 964 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 425a, 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 252.}}
**6** of wrestlers, **have a fall**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 590; πεσών γε κείσομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 126.}}
**II** of places, **to be situated, lie**, νῆσος ἀπόπροθεν εἰν ἁλὶ κεῖται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.244, compare 9.25, 10.196}}, etc.; ἐν τῇ [γῇ] κείμενά ἐστι τὰ Σοῦσα (for κεῖται) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.49; Αἴγινα.. πρὸς νότου κ. πνοάς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 404; πρὸ Μεγάρων κ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.51; πόλις αὐτάρκη θέσιν κειμένη [prev. author] 1.37; θέσιν κέεσθαι νοσερωτάτην 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aër.” 6,compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 496a14}}; κ. πρὸς τὸν ἥλιον, πρὸς ἄρκτον, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mete.” 360b14, 363a3.}}
**II.2** of things, **lie** or **be** in a place, ὅθι οἱ φίλα δέμνι᾽ ἔκειτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.277}}; ἕλε δίφρον κείμενον **placed there**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|17.331, compare 410; φόρμιγγα.., ἥ που κεῖται ἐν ἡμετέροισι δόμοισι 8.255: in Prose, δύο τράπεζαι ἐκείσθην 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.37; χύτρας εὐκρινῶς κειμένας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 8.19.}}
**II.3** **fit**, of shoes, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.121.}}
**II.4** κεῖσθαι, **posture, attitude**, as a category, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 2a2.}}
**III** **to be laid up, in store**, of goods, property, etc., δόμοις ἐν κτήματα κεῖται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.382; πολλὰ δ᾽ ἐν ἀφνειοῦ πατρὸς κειμήλια κ. 6.47}}; βασιλῆϊ δὲ κεῖται ἄγαλμα **is reserved**.., {{Refs|4.144}}; μνῆμα ξείνοιο.. κέσκετ᾽ ἐνὶ μεγάροισι **was left lying**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.41}}; of things dedicated to a god, κ. ἐν θησαυρῷ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.51, compare 52, 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 94}}; of money, κείμενα **deposits**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.86. ; κ. σοι εὐεργεσία ἐν τῷ ἡμετέρῳ οἴκῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.129, compare “SIG” 22.15 (Epist. Darei), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 345a}}; πολλὰ χρήματα ἐπὶ τῇ τούτου τραπέζῃ κεῖταί μοι at his bank, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.44; παρά τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 346c}}; τἀργύριόν σοι κείσεται the caution-money **shall be deposited**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 624}}; δραχ μὴν ὑπόθες.—Answ. κεῖται πάλαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 73.2: _metaphorically_, εἰ ταῦτ᾽ ἀνατὶ τῇδε κείσεται κράτη}} **shall be placed to** her **credit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 485, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).18.}}
**IV** **to be placed in position**, τῶν ἐπὶ τοῦ τοίχου.. κειμένων κιόνων {{Refs|“IG” 12.372.46.}}
**IV.2** **to be set up, ordained**, ἄεθλα κεῖτ᾽ ἐν ἀγῶνι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.273, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.26, 93, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.46; ὅπλων ἔκειτ᾽ ἀγὼν πέρι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 936 (Lyric poetry).}}
**IV.3** of laws, κεῖται νόμος the law **is laid down**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 292; νόμοι ἐπ᾽ ὠφελίᾳ τῶν ἀδικουμένων κεῖνται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.37; νόμοι κεῖνται περί τινος 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.2}}; οἱ νόμοι οἱ κείμενοι the **established** laws, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 914, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.48, etc.; οἱ ὑπὸ τῶν θεῶν κείμενοι νόμοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.21; οἱ νόμοι οἱ ὑπὸ οἱ τῶν βασιλέων κείμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.36, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.62; καινὰ κεῖσθαι θέσμι᾽ ἀνθρώποις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 494}}; αἱ κείμεναι ὑπὸ τῶν ὑπατικῶν γνῶμαι the votes **given** by.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 7.47}}; οὐκέτι κ. ἡ διαθήκη no longer **holds**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 6.32}}; so of philosophical arguments, **hold good**, κατά τινων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.51 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; θάνατος ὧν κεῖται πέρι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 750; κείμεναι ζημίαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.9, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45.}}
**IV.4** **to be laid down in argument, posited, assumed**, τοῦτο ἡμῖν οὕτω κείσθω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 350d, etc.; ὡμολογημένον ἡμῖν κ. [prev. author] “Plt.” 300e; frequently in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, κείσθω}}**let it be assumed, **{{Refs|“Apr.” 34b23, +others}}; τὸ ἐξ ἀρχῆς κείμενον the _assumption, _{{Refs|“Metaphorical” 1008b2, 1047b10}}(_plural_); τὰ περὶ τὴν διάνοιαν ἐν τοῖς περὶ ῥητορικῆς κ. {{Refs|“Po.” 1456a35.}}
**IV.5** of names, οὔνομα κεῖται the name **is given**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.184, 7.200, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.12, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 257c, etc.}}; ὑπὸ τοῦ πατρὸς κείμενον [ὄνομα] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.32}}; κεῖσθαι without ὄνομα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 392d}}; κείμενα ὀόματα **established** terms, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 140a3, 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 96.}}
**IV.6** _metaphorically_, πάντα δεινὰ κἀπικινδύνως βροτοῖς κεῖται, παθεῖν μὲν εὖ, παθεῖν δὲ θάτερα danger **is set before** men, that they may.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 503.}}
**V** _metaphorically_, of continuing conditions, ἐνὶ φρεσὶ πένθος ἔκειτο **lay heavy**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.423}}; εὔστομα κείσθω **remain** unspoken, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.171}}; νεῖκος ἔκειτό τισι there **was** an **enduring** feud, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 491 (Lyric poetry)}}; Ἑλλήνων κείσομαι ἐν στόματι my name **shall be a household word,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|Anthologia Graeca 9.62 (5th c.BC: Evenus Elegiacus); πολλῶν κείμενος ἐν στόμασιν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 240; εὖ κείμενα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 693}}; μὴ κινεῖν (i.e. κακὸν) εὖ κείμενον 'let sleeping dogs lie', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 15c, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 30, 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}
**V.2** ταῦτα θεῶν ἐν γούνασι κεῖται, i.e. these things **are** yet **in the power of** the gods, to give or not, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.514, 20.435.}}
**V.3** κεῖσθαι ἔν τινι **to rest entirely** or **be dependent** on him, ἐν ἀγαθοῖσι κ. πολίων κυβερνάσιες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.71; ἐν ὔμμι ὡς θεῷ κείμεθα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 248 (Lyric poetry); also ἐπί τινι, τὰ δ᾽ οὐκ ἐπ᾽ ἀνδράσι κ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.76}}: also with simple _dative_, Λεωφίλῳ πάντα κεῖται {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 69, probably in “Comica Adespota” 1325}}; of things, **depend upon**, τὸ πανηγυρικὸν ἐν μελέτῃ καὶ τριβῇ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.93 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; τὰ.. γυμνάσια ἐν τῇ κινήσει κ. 2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 6.23.1.}}
**V.4** in Medicine texts, **to be left to settle**, of urine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.26.β.}}
**V.4.b** φάρυγξ οὐ φλεγμαίνουσα, κειμένη δέ{{Refs|i.e. not swollen, [prev. work] 2.2.24.}}
**V.5** Grammars, of words and phrases, **to be found, occur**, παρὰ τῷ ποιητῃ {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 7.3.6, compare 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.58b; κεῖται ἐν τῷ Περὶ Πλούτου 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.39 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}; ποῦ κεῖτα; {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.165d}}, compare Κειτούκειτο; κ. ἀντί τινος **to be used** instead of.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 8.6.7}}; τὸ κείμενον the **received text**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia vulgar form 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.48.}}

2750:
** κειρία**, ἡ,
**girth of a bedstead**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 816, LXX.Pro.7.16, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 16.}}
**II** **swathing-band, bandage**, written **κηρία**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PMed.Lond.” 155 IV10, 28, +others, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.83, 2.59, 61, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (-ρεί-)}}; **καιρία** (which see); **κιρία**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 69.9, 11, “PSI” 4.341.7, 387.4 (3rd c.BC)}}; **grave-clothes**, in form κειρίαι (variant{κηρίαι}) {{NT|NT.John.11.44.}}
**III** κηρίαι, **tapeworms**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus cited in 1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus; κειρίαι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.755.}}

2751:
** κείρω**,
_future_ κερῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 471a}}, _Ionic dialect_ κερέω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.146}}: _aorist_ ἔκειρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.37, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1173, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ ἔκερσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.546 (ἀπο-, in tmesi), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 666 (Lyric poetry)}}: _perfect_ κέκαρκα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Sammelb.” 6002 (2nd c.BC), (περι-) 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Symp.” 32}}:—_middle_, _future_ κεροῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1183, (ἀπο-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89b}}: _aorist_ ἐκειράμἡν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.60, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ ἐκερσάμην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 311, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 953 (Lyric poetry):}} —_passive_, _aorist 1_ _participle_ κερθείς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(variant καρθ-) 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.82:}}_aorist 2_ ἐκάρην [ᾰ] {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.368.45 (3rd c.BC)}}, _subjunctive_ κᾰρῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.127}}_infinitive_ κᾰρῆναι, _participle_ καρείς, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 6, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lysander” 1}}: _perfect_ _infinitive_ κεκάρθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.36}}: _Attic dialect_ _pluperfect_ ἐκεκάρμην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Lex.” 5}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _kṛṇā´ti_ 'wound', Latin _caro_: probably also O{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **scieran**, Eng. **shear.):—cut short, shear, clip**, especially of hair, σοί τε κόμην κερέειν (i.e. Σπερχειῷ) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.146, compare 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.37.3}}; κ. ἐν χρ [τὰς τρίχας] **crop** it close, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.175; ἀλόχων κείραντες ἔθειραν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1124 (Lyric poetry)}}: —more frequently in _middle_, **cut off one's** hair or **have** it **cut off**, as a sign of mourning (compare κουρά), τοῦτο.. γέρας οἶον ὀϊζυροῖσι βροτοῖσι, κείρασθαί τε κόμην βαλέειν τ᾽ ἀπὸ δάκρυ παρειῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.198, compare 24.46, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.46; πολύν σοι βοστρύχων πλόκαμον κεροῦμαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1183; κείρομαι κόμαν [prev. author] “Ph.” 322 (Lyric poetry)}}: _absolutely_, **cut off one's hair**, κείρασθε, συμπενθήσατ᾽ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “HF” 1390; ἐφ᾽ οἷς ἡ πόλις ἐπένθησε καὶ ἐκείρατο 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.211, etc.; ἄξιον ἦν ἐπὶ τῷδε τῷ τάφῳ κείρασθαι τῇ Ἑλλάδι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator [same place]: Comedy texts, πρὸς φθεῖρα κείρασθαι}} **to have oneself** close **shorn**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 32}}:—_passive_, κουρᾷ.. πενθίμῳ κεκαρμένος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 458}}; σύμβολον κ. half-**cropped**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 14; τὰ ῥόδα κ. 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 108.29}}; also, of the hair, **to be cut off**, πλόκαμοι κερθέντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.82; βοστρύχους κεκαρμένους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 515.}}
**2** **crop** a person, σφέας αὐτοὺς καὶ τοὺς ἵππους, in sign of mourning, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.24}}; κεκάρθαι τὰς κεφαλάς **to have** their heads **shorn**, [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.36; Θρᾳκιστὶ κέκαρμαι 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.46; see at {χρώς} I.2, ἐγκυτί}}]; **shear** sheep, μάχαιραι κουρίδες, αἷς κείρομεν τὰ πρόβατα καὶ τοὺς ποιμένας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 37}}; κείρεσθαί (**tonderi**) μου τὰ πρόβατα, ἀλλ᾽ οὐκ ἀποξύρεσθαι (**deglubi**) βούλομαι Tiber. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 57.10 (compare below 3)}}; τὸ μὲν [καρῆναι] ἐπὶ προβάτων τιθέασι καὶ ἐπὶ ἀτίμου κουρᾶς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare (2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 6), κείρασθαι δὲ ἐπὶ ἀνθρώπων 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 292}}; but τῶν Ἀργείων ἐπὶ πένθει καρέντων Plu.[same place]; τῷ σε χρὴ δρεπάνοισι καὶ οὐ ψαλίδεσσι καρῆναι {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.368 (4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator Antec.).}}
**3** _metaphorically_, '**fleece', plunder**, τὴν μάμμην {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.39.}}
**II** **cut down**, δοῦρ᾽ ἐλάτης κέρσαντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.450; ὕλην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1196}}; **crop close**, opposed to ἐπιτέμνειν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.23.3}}; **pluck**, ἄνθη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.5: _metaphorically_, ἐκ λεχέων κ. μελιαδέα ποίαν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.37; Ἄρης κέρσειεν ἄωτον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 666 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.2** **ravage** a country, especially **by cutting down** crops and fruit-trees, τὸ πεδίον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.63; τέμενος [prev. author] 6.75, compare “OGI” 765.10 (from Priene)}}; τὴν γῆν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.99, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.64; χώραν 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 15.9}}; **destroy**, πόλιν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Fragmenta” 1.60}} P.; also, **clear**, of pioneers, ὄρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.131}}:—_passive_, of a country, **to be ravaged**, καρῆναι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.127, compare 8.65; κεκαρμένα κτήματα “SIG” 364.67 (Ephesus, 3rd c.BC):}} —_middle_, χθὼν πεύκας κειραμένη **having its** pine-trees **cut down,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 9.106 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus); ἄρουραι λήϊα κειράμεναι 1st c.AD(?): Pseudo-Phocylidea 166: _metaphorically_, Σπάρτη.. ἐκείρατο δόξαν}} **had her** glory **shorn off**, epigram cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 9.15.6}}; Ἄρης νυχίαν πλάκα κερσάμενος **having had** the plain **swept clean** (by destroying the men), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 953 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.3** ἔκειρε πολύκερων φόνον **slaughtered** many a horned beast **by hewing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 55.}}
**II.4** **hew, carve**, ἐπίβασιν {{Refs|“Inscription Cyprian dialect” 99 H.}}
**II.5** **cut through transversely**, opposed to σχίζειν (slit longitudinally), φλέβα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 7.11.3.}}
**III** generally, **destroy, consume**:
**III.1** **tear, eat greedily**, of beasts, κείρει τ᾽ εἰσελθὼν βαθὺ λήϊον [ὄνος] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.560}}; of fish, δημὸν.. ἐπινεφρίδιον κείροντες {{Refs|21.204}}; of vultures, ἧπαρ ἔκειρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.578, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 1.1, “DMort.” 30.1.}}
**III.2** _metaphorically_, **waste, devour**, ἐκείρετε πολλὰ καὶ ἐσθλὰ κτήματ᾽ ἐμά {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.312; ἔκειρον κτήματ᾽ ἐνὶ μεγάρῳ 22.369, etc.: _absolutely_, κείρετε (i.e. βίοτον) 1.378.}}

2753:
** κελεύω**,
_Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ κέλευον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.767}}: _future_ **κελεύ-σω**, _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ - σέμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.274:}}_aorist_ ἐκέλευσα, _Epic dialect_ κέλ- {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.4}}: _perfect_ κεκέλευκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.34, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Demon.” 44}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ ἐκελευσάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 13: more frequently in compounds δια-, ἐπι-, παρα-κελεύομαι (which see)}}:—_passive_, _future_ -ευσθήσομαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 68.9}}: _aorist_ ἐκελεύσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.9.ά, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 738, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.70}}: _perfect_ κεκέλευσμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.14, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sacr.” 11}}: _pluperfect_ ἐκεκέλευστο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 78.4 (ἐκελεύθην variant in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.9.ά, and κεκέλευμαι “IG” 22.1121.13 (4th c.AD), variant in 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.141 are later forms). (A lengthened form of κέλομαι, which see)}}:—properly **urge, drive on**, [ἵππους] ὁ γέρων ἐφέπων μάστιγι κέλευε.. κατὰ ἄστυ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.326}}: hence, **exhort, bid**,
**1** c._accusative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, **order** one to do, σ᾽ ἔγωγε.. κελεύω ἐς πληθὺν ἰέναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|17.30, compare 2.11, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8, 24, etc.}}; ἐκέλευσε τὸν παῖδα περιμεῖναί ἑ κελεῦσαι he **bade** the lad **bid** us to wait for him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 327b}}; ὁ νόμος τὸν ἐπιβουλεύσαντα κελεύει φονέα εἶναι, i.e. **bids that** he be held guilty, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.2.5; ὁ τὸν νόμον κελεύων ἄρχειν δοκεῖ κελεύειν ἄρχειν τὸν θεὸν καὶ τὸν νοῦν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287a29; ἐς τὴν Μίλητον ἔπεμπον κελεύοντες σφίσι τὸν Ἀστύοχον βοηθεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.38}}; **request**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.16}}; opposed to ἐπιτάττειν, {{Refs|“IG” 12.76.33.}}
**2** with _accusative_ _person_ et _of things_, σφῶϊ μὲν οὔ τι κελεύω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.286; τά με θυμὸς.. κελεύει (i.e. εἰπεῖν) 7.68, etc.: with}}_infinitive_ subjoined, τί με ταῦτα κελεύεις.. μάχεσθα; {{Refs|20.87.}}
**3** with _accusative_ _person_ only, εἰ μὴ θυμός με κελεύοι (i.e. φείδεσθαι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.278; ὥς με κελεύεις (i.e. μυθεῖσθαι) 11.507: in Prose, ἐκέλευσε τοὺς ἕνδεκα ἐπὶ τὸν Θηραμένην}} **ordered** them [to go] against him, **ordered** them to seize him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.54; κ. τινὰς ἐπὶ τὰ ὅπλα [prev. work] 20}}:—_passive_, **receive orders**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1253b34.}}
**4** c._accusative_ _of things_ only, ὃ μὴ κελεύσαι Ζεύς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(2nd-3rd c.AD: Hermes Trismegistus for -σει) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 618; ὁ νόμος τὰ μὲν κελεύων τὰ δ᾽ ἀπαγορεύων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1129b24:}} — _passive_, τὸ κελευόμενον **commands, orders**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.16, 5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 61, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.3: _plural_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 340a.}}
**5** with _dative_ _person_ followed by _infinitive_, **urge** or **order** one to do, κηρύκεσσι.. κέλευσε κηρύσσειν.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.50, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.6, etc.}}; ἀλλήλοισι κέλευον ἅπτεσθαι νηῶν.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.151; ἑτάροισι.. ἐκέλευσα ἐμβαλέειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.488: in later Prose, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.17, 1st c.AD: Cebes Philosophus 32.4 codices, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 1.1, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 121.1, etc.}}
**6** rarely with _dative_ _person_ et _accusative_ _of things_, τί δ᾽ ἐστὶν ὃ κελεύεις ἐμο; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 224, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 9.1.}}
**7** with _dative_ _person_ only, ἵπποισι καὶ Αὐτομέδοντι κελεύσας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.684; compareinfr. 111.}}
**8** _absolutely_, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ὡς σὺ κελεύεις 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.96, +others; λέξω, κελεύεις γάρ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 107}}; κελεύων, opposed to αὐτοχειρίῃ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 260; κελευούσης τῆς Πυθίης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.36; κελεύοντος καὶ δεομένου 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 5.1.}}
**9** with _infinitive_ only, σιγᾶν κελεύω I **order** silence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 865; οὐκ ἂν κελεύσαιμ᾽ εὐσεβεῖν [prev. author] “Ant.” 731}}; **recommend, propose**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.25, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.21, etc.}}; opposed to οὐκ ἐάω, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 2.18.}}
**II** of inferiors, **urge, entreat**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.599, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.17, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.116.}}
**III** of the boatswain, **give time** to rowers, c._dative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 396b: _absolutely_, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.535d.}}
**III.2** **sing a chanty**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 6.24.}}

2754:
** κενοδοξ-ία**, ἡ,
**liability to vain imagination**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 30, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.332 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**II** **vanity, conceit**, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.81.9, LXX.Wis.14.14, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.107, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.47, NT.Phil.2.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.57d, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 15}}; especially of **false prudery**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.415.}}

2756:
** κενός**, ή, όν,
_Ionic dialect_ and _poetry_ **κεινός** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.376, 4.181, 11.160, 15.453, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.65, 3.45, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.73, +others}}; _Epic dialect_ also **κενεός**, as always in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} (except in Il.[same places], and κενός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.249 (assuming variant, perhaps κενέ᾽ εὔγματα or κείν᾽ εὔγματα)), also 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 7.24, 5th c.BC: Melissus Philosophus 7, Timo 20.2 (_comparative_)}}, and in _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).121.73 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **κέννος**, _accusative_ to Greg. Cor.p.610 {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus: _superlative_ κεννότατος Scholia Tz.in “Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 3.356.18}}; but οἱ Αἰολεῖς.. οὐ λέγουσι κέννος Choerob.in {{Refs|“Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 2.242, cf. Hdn.Gr.2.302, and the true _Aeolic dialect_ is probably κένος or κένεος}}, from κενϝος, kenevos,{{Refs 5th c.AD+|compare 5th c.AD: Cypria}} **κενευϝός** {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 683.4.}}
**I** mostly of things, **empty**, opposed to πλέως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 280}}; opposed to πλήρης, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Nu.” 1054}}; opposed to μεστός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 12; κενεὰς σὺν χεῖρας ἔχοντες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.42; νοστήσαντας κεινῇσι χερσί 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.73; κεναῖς χερσίν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 796b (see. below 11.2)}}; τὸ κ. (i.e. τάλαντον) the **empty** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 488.5; κ. οἴκησις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 31; γῆ [prev. author] “OT” 55; εὐνή [prev. author] “Ant.” 424}}; χώματα κεινά, ={κενοτάφια}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.85; κ. τάφος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1057}}; κατέθισαν ἐπὶ κενευϝῶν (i.e. τάφων _genitive_ _singular_) {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“Schwyzer” [prev. cited] (5th c.AD: Cypria)}}; κ. χρόνος a **pause** in music, Anon.{{Refs 1st c.AD+|Bellerm. 83; σφυγμὸς κ. 1st c.AD: Agathinus Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.936}}; of wool and wine, uncertain meaning in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.945}}; τὸ κ. **the void of space**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 9, Meliss.[same place], 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 13, +others, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.6U., etc.}}; τὸ κ., ={τόπος ἐστερημένος σώματος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 208b26, compare 213a13 ff., “Cael.” 279a13; κ. χώρα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 58a}}; ἢν κενεὸν λάβῃ [ἡ διακοπή] if it penetrates the (brain-) **cavity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aph.”}}[same place]; especiallyAstrology texts, **not occupied by a planet**, κ. δρόμος {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 2.452, compareVett. Val. 94.27}}; compare κενοδρομέω.
**I.2** **empty, fruitless, void**, κενὰ εὔγματα εἰπών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.249 (see. above)}}; ἐλπίς, ἐλπίδες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 5.16, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 804; γνώμα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.40, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 753; ἔξοδοι [prev. author] “Aj.” 287; φροντίδες [prev. author] “fragment” 949}}; τέρψις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 577; φόβοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 548, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.21; φρόνημα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 494d, etc.; κ. πρόφασις καὶ ψευδής 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.150; λοιδορία κ. [prev. author] 2.5; μάταιον καὶ κ. [prev. work]12; κενὸν ἆρα καὶ τὸ φάρμακον πρὸς τὸ κ. probably in 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 530.19}}; ἀπόντων κενὴν κατηγορεῖν bring an **idle** charge, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Respiratione” 470b12}}; **ineffectual**, λύγξ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49}}; πουλυμαθημοσύνης, τῆς οὐ κενεώτερον ἄλλο Timo [same place]; πολλὰ κ. τοῦ πολέμου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1116b7; κ. δόξαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 15}}; ἐπιθυμίαι, opposed to φυσικαί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ep.” 3p.62U., 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 59; κ. ὀρέξεις 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 831.16; frequently in adverbial usages, _neuter_ _plural_, κενεὰ πνεύσαις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).93}}; ἡ διὰ κενῆς ἐπανάσεισις τῶν ὅπλων **empty** flourishing of arms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.126}}; διὰ κενῆς ῥίπτειν throw **without a projectile**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 881a39}}; κεκλάγγω διὰ κενῆς ἄλλως **to no purpose**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 929; μάτην διὰ κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 174.21; οὐ μαχοῦμαί σοι διὰ κ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 260; ἐν κενοῖς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 971; κατὰ κενῆς 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Timaeum commentarii” 2.167 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; εἰς κενόν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.9, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 10.30; εἰς κ. ἡ δαπάνη “IG” 14.1746; εἰς κ. μοχθεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 51}}; κατὰ κενοῦ χανεῖν Suid. see at {λύκος ἔχανε; κατὰ κενοῦ φέρειν τὰς χεῖρας} {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.153}}; κατὰ κ. βαίνειν, ={κενεμβατεῖν}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.463c}}: regular _adverb_ κενῶς, διαλεκτικῶς καὶ κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 403a2; λογικῶς καὶ κ. [prev. author] “EE” 1217b21; μὴ κ. πόνει 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 1101, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.61U., 3rd c.BC: Polystratus Epicureus p.7 W., 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.17.6, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.35e.}}
**II** of persons and things,
**II.1** c._genitive_, **destitute, bereft**, τοῦ νοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 931; φρενῶν [prev. author] “Ant.” 754; δακρύων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 230; συμμάχων κ. δόρυ [prev. author] “Or.” 688; πεδίον κ. δένδρων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 621a}}; κ. φρονήσεως, ἐπιστήμης, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 75a, “R.” 486c}}; κ. πόνου **without the fruits** of toil, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 241.}}
**II.2** _absolutely_, **empty-handed**, αἰσχρόν τοι δηρόν τε μενειν κενεόν τε νέεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.298, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.214}}; ἀπίκατο, οἱ μὲν κεινοί, οἱ δὲ φέροντες κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.131; κενὸς κενὸν καλεῖ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 353 (Lyric poetry); ἥκεις οὐ κενή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 359, compare “Tr.” 495; οὐθ᾽ ὑπεργέμων.. οὔτε κ. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 216}}; of camels, **without burdens, unloaded**, opposed to ἔγγομοι, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 629.166 (Pal- myra, 2nd c.AD)}}; κ. ἂν ἴῃ.., κ. ἄπεισιν {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 370e; κ. τινὰ ἐξαποστεῖλαι LXX.Gen.31.42}}; **bereft of** her mate, λέαινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 986}}; **orphan**, Ἔρωτες {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus 1.59}}; ὑπ᾽ ἄσθματος κενοί **exhausted**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 484}}; of places, **without garrison**, χῶραι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.146, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.15}}; of the body, **without flesh**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.831c.}}
**II.2.b** **devoid of wit, vain, pretentious**, κεινὸς εἴην {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.45; διαπτυχθέντες ὤφθησαν κενοί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 709; ἀνόητον καὶ κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 530, cf. NT.Jam.2.20.}}
**III** _comparative_ and _superlative_, κενότερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 10 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; -ότατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.25, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.67 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, +others, compare 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.76, “Etymologicum Magnum” 275.50}}; κενώτερος, -ώτατος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 175d, variant in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1107a30 (_comparative_)}}; κενεώτερος Timo (see. above); κενεώτατος variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 62.}}

2758:
** κενόω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 959, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 197d}}; _Epic dialect_ **κεινόω** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 56, “Al.” 140}}: _future_ κενώσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 447}}: _aorist_ ἐκένωσα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ba.” 730}}: _perfect_ κεκένωκα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.67}}:—_passive_, _future_ κενωθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 4.709, κενεώσομαι 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 16}}: _aorist_ ἐκενώθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51}}: _perfect_ κεκένωμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.123, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἱερῆς νούσου” 9}}: (κενός): —**empty**, πᾶσαν ἠπείρου πλάκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 718 (troch.); ναούς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion”}}[same place]: with _genitive_, **empty of** a thing, ἀνδρῶν τάνδε πόλιν κενῶσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 660, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 914 (Lyric poetry)}}; χέρας [δώρων] {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. author]“Med.”[same place]; τινὰ τᾶς συοπλουτοσύνας 3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 4.13}}; opposed to πληροῦν τινά τινος, Pl.[same place],{{Refs|compare “R.” 560d}}:—_passive_, **to be emptied, made** or **left empty**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 29}}; ἐς τὸ κενούμενον into **the space continually left empty**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76; οἰκίαι πολλαὶ ἐκενώθησαν [prev. work] 51: with _genitive_, τούτων κενεώσεται.. αἰών}} **will be left without** them, Emp.[same place]; κεκενωμένου τοῦ τείχεος πάντων **stripped** of all things, Hdt. [prev. cited]
**2** **make** a place **empty by leaving** it, **desert** it, βωμοῦ ἐσχάραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1138}}; λόχμην {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ba.”}}[same place]:—_passive_, κενωθεισῶν τῶν νεῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.57.}}
**3** in Medicine texts, **empty by depletion**, opposed to πληροῦν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.51, compare 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.2}}, Gal.[same place]; τινα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.30}} O.; **carry off**, αἷμα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Ocyp.” 93; ἐκ τοῦ σώματος χολήν 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ φυσικῶν δυνάμεων - Scripta Minora” 1.13:}} —_passive_, τὰ κενούμενα **evacuations**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] 6.78, 2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.37.27.}}
**4** **empty out, pour away**, φάρμακον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Iamblichus Scriptor Eroticus 7: _metaphorically_, πλοῦτον}} _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.119}}:—_passive_, τοῦ λαοῦ κενωθέντος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 24.1}}; **make away with**, θανάτου βάρος {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria “fragment” 1.6.}}
**5** **expend**, εἴς με κένωσον πᾶν βέλος {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.57 (1st c.BC: Archias).}}
**6** in _passive_, **waste away, shrivel**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.4.3, 9.14.3.}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **make empty**, ἑαυτόν {{NT|NT.Phil.2.7}}; **make void** or **of no effect**, καύχημα {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.1Cor.9.15; ὑπάρξεις 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 90.7}}:—_passive_, **to be** or **become** so, {{NT|NT.Rom.4.14.}}

2759:
** κέντρον**, τό,
(κεντέω) **any sharp point**:
**1** **horse-goad**, [ἵπποι] ἄνευ κέντροιο θέοντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.387, compare 430, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1297, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.29, etc.; διπλοῖς κέντροισι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 809; ὄνειδος ἔτυψεν δίκαν διφρηλάτου μεσολαβεῖ κ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 157 (Lyric poetry): post- 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **ox-goad** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus βουπλήξ)}}, used as an instrument of torture, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.130; κέντροις καὶ μάστιξιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 777a: proverbial, πρὸς κέντρα λακτίζειν (see. λακτίζω 2); δεῖ.. κέντρου πολλάκις, οὕτω δὲ καὶ χαλινοῦ 3rd c.AD: Longinus Rhetor 2.2}}; as a symbol of sovereignty, λαβὼν.. χερσὶν κέντρα κηδεύει πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 683.}}
**1.b** _metaphorically_, **goad, spur, incentive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 124.4, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 691 (Lyric poetry)}}; ποῦ γὰρ τοσοῦτο κ. ὡς μητροκτονεῖ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eu.” 427; κέντροις ἔρωτος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 39, compare 1303; πόθου κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 573a; κέντρα καὶ ὠδῖνες [prev. author] “Phdr.” 251e; κ. ἐγερτικὸν θυμοῦ 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 21}}; κέντρα πτολέμοιο, of the Argives, Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|Scholia 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.48}}; κ. ἐμοῦ **desire for** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1039.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, in _plural_, **tortures, pangs**, {{NT|[prev. author] “Tr.” 840 (Lyric poetry): _sing._, τὸ κ. τοῦ θανάτου ἡ ἁμαρτία NT.1Cor.15.56.}}
**3** **point** of a spear, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.22.4: _plural_, of the περόναι}} with which Oedipus pierced his eyes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1318.}}
**4** **peg** of a top, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 436d.}}
**5** of animals,
**5.a** **sting** of bees and wasps, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 225, 407 (Lyric poetry), +others}}; of scorpions, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 683a12}} (so of the constellation {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Scorpio”, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 505)}}: hence, _metaphorically_, of malicious persons, ἐς τοὺς ἔχοντας κέντρ᾽ ἀφιᾶσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 242; πορεύεται, ὥσπερ σκορπίος, ἠρκὼς τὸ κ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.52}}; of Pericles as an orator, τὸ κ. ἐγκατέλειπε τοῖς ἀκροωμένοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 94.7}}; of Socrates, ὥσπερ μέλιττα τὸ κ. ἐγκαταλιπών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 91c; οἷον ὀφθαλμῷ κ. ἐνθεῖσα 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 2.1}}; βλέμματος κ. Onomarch. cited in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.18.}}
**5.b** **spur** of a cock, {{Refs|“Geoponica” 14.7.17.}}
**5.c** **quill** of the porcupine, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 12.26.}}
**5.d** ={πόσθη}, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Sotades Lyricus 1.}}
**6** **stationary point of a pair of compasses**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Vitruvius Scriptor de Architectura Latinus 3.1.3}}: generally, **centre** of a circle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 54e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 41b15, +others}}; ἡ ἐκ τοῦ κ. (i.e. εὐθεῖα) **radius**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra “Optica” 34; ὥσπερ κύκλον κέντρῳ περιέγραψαν τὴν πόλιν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 11; τὸ κ. τᾶς σφαίρας 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 100e; τὸ κ. τῆς γῆς 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 52}}; κ. βάρεος **centre** of gravity, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἰσορροπιῶν” 1 “Def.” 4: _metaphorically_, κ. καὶ διαστήματι περιγράφειν}} **circumscribe**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.513c, 524f.}}
**7** **pin, rivet**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.16.1}}; **spur, tip**, for fixing a machine in the ground, {{Refs|Apollod “Poliorc.” 144.1.}}
**8** ῥακτηρίοις κέντροισιν, of **oars**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 802.}}
**9** Astron., **cardinal point** on the ecliptic, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 74, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 5.12, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 50.18, etc.}}
**10** **hard knot** in stone, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.2.3}}; **flaw** in crystals, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 37.28.}}

2761:
Included with: ** κενός**, ή, όν,
_Ionic dialect_ and _poetry_ **κεινός** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.376, 4.181, 11.160, 15.453, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.65, 3.45, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.73, +others}}; _Epic dialect_ also **κενεός**, as always in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} (except in Il.[same places], and κενός {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.249 (assuming variant, perhaps κενέ᾽ εὔγματα or κείν᾽ εὔγματα)), also 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 7.24, 5th c.BC: Melissus Philosophus 7, Timo 20.2 (_comparative_)}}, and in _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 42(1).121.73 (Epid., 4th c.BC)}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **κέννος**, _accusative_ to Greg. Cor.p.610 {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus: _superlative_ κεννότατος Scholia Tz.in “Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 3.356.18}}; but οἱ Αἰολεῖς.. οὐ λέγουσι κέννος Choerob.in {{Refs|“Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 2.242, cf. Hdn.Gr.2.302, and the true _Aeolic dialect_ is probably κένος or κένεος}}, from κενϝος, kenevos,{{Refs 5th c.AD+|compare 5th c.AD: Cypria}} **κενευϝός** {{Refs|“Schwyzer” 683.4.}}
**I** mostly of things, **empty**, opposed to πλέως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 280}}; opposed to πλήρης, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Nu.” 1054}}; opposed to μεστός, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 12; κενεὰς σὺν χεῖρας ἔχοντες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.42; νοστήσαντας κεινῇσι χερσί 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.73; κεναῖς χερσίν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 796b (see. below 11.2)}}; τὸ κ. (i.e. τάλαντον) the **empty** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 488.5; κ. οἴκησις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 31; γῆ [prev. author] “OT” 55; εὐνή [prev. author] “Ant.” 424}}; χώματα κεινά, ={κενοτάφια}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.85; κ. τάφος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1057}}; κατέθισαν ἐπὶ κενευϝῶν (i.e. τάφων _genitive_ _singular_) {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“Schwyzer” [prev. cited] (5th c.AD: Cypria)}}; κ. χρόνος a **pause** in music, Anon.{{Refs 1st c.AD+|Bellerm. 83; σφυγμὸς κ. 1st c.AD: Agathinus Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.936}}; of wool and wine, uncertain meaning in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.945}}; τὸ κ. **the void of space**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 9, Meliss.[same place], 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 13, +others, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.6U., etc.}}; τὸ κ., ={τόπος ἐστερημένος σώματος}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 208b26, compare 213a13 ff., “Cael.” 279a13; κ. χώρα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 58a}}; ἢν κενεὸν λάβῃ [ἡ διακοπή] if it penetrates the (brain-) **cavity**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aph.”}}[same place]; especiallyAstrology texts, **not occupied by a planet**, κ. δρόμος {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 2.452, compareVett. Val. 94.27}}; compare κενοδρομέω.
**I.2** **empty, fruitless, void**, κενὰ εὔγματα εἰπών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.249 (see. above)}}; ἐλπίς, ἐλπίδες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 5.16, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 804; γνώμα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.40, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 753; ἔξοδοι [prev. author] “Aj.” 287; φροντίδες [prev. author] “fragment” 949}}; τέρψις{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 577; φόβοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 548, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.21; φρόνημα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 494d, etc.; κ. πρόφασις καὶ ψευδής 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.150; λοιδορία κ. [prev. author] 2.5; μάταιον καὶ κ. [prev. work]12; κενὸν ἆρα καὶ τὸ φάρμακον πρὸς τὸ κ. probably in 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 530.19}}; ἀπόντων κενὴν κατηγορεῖν bring an **idle** charge, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Respiratione” 470b12}}; **ineffectual**, λύγξ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49}}; πουλυμαθημοσύνης, τῆς οὐ κενεώτερον ἄλλο Timo [same place]; πολλὰ κ. τοῦ πολέμου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1116b7; κ. δόξαι 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Sententiae” 15}}; ἐπιθυμίαι, opposed to φυσικαί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ep.” 3p.62U., 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 59; κ. ὀρέξεις 4th c.AD: Metrodorus Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 831.16; frequently in adverbial usages, _neuter_ _plural_, κενεὰ πνεύσαις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).93}}; ἡ διὰ κενῆς ἐπανάσεισις τῶν ὅπλων **empty** flourishing of arms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.126}}; διὰ κενῆς ῥίπτειν throw **without a projectile**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 881a39}}; κεκλάγγω διὰ κενῆς ἄλλως **to no purpose**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 929; μάτην διὰ κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 174.21; οὐ μαχοῦμαί σοι διὰ κ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 260; ἐν κενοῖς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 971; κατὰ κενῆς 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Timaeum commentarii” 2.167 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; εἰς κενόν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.9, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 10.30; εἰς κ. ἡ δαπάνη “IG” 14.1746; εἰς κ. μοχθεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 51}}; κατὰ κενοῦ χανεῖν Suid. see at {λύκος ἔχανε; κατὰ κενοῦ φέρειν τὰς χεῖρας} {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.153}}; κατὰ κ. βαίνειν, ={κενεμβατεῖν}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.463c}}: regular _adverb_ κενῶς, διαλεκτικῶς καὶ κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Anima” 403a2; λογικῶς καὶ κ. [prev. author] “EE” 1217b21; μὴ κ. πόνει 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 1101, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 3p.61U., 3rd c.BC: Polystratus Epicureus p.7 W., 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.17.6, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.35e.}}
**II** of persons and things,
**II.1** c._genitive_, **destitute, bereft**, τοῦ νοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 931; φρενῶν [prev. author] “Ant.” 754; δακρύων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 230; συμμάχων κ. δόρυ [prev. author] “Or.” 688; πεδίον κ. δένδρων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 621a}}; κ. φρονήσεως, ἐπιστήμης, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ti.” 75a, “R.” 486c}}; κ. πόνου **without the fruits** of toil, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 241.}}
**II.2** _absolutely_, **empty-handed**, αἰσχρόν τοι δηρόν τε μενειν κενεόν τε νέεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.298, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.214}}; ἀπίκατο, οἱ μὲν κεινοί, οἱ δὲ φέροντες κτλ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.131; κενὸς κενὸν καλεῖ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 353 (Lyric poetry); ἥκεις οὐ κενή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 359, compare “Tr.” 495; οὐθ᾽ ὑπεργέμων.. οὔτε κ. 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 216}}; of camels, **without burdens, unloaded**, opposed to ἔγγομοι, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 629.166 (Pal- myra, 2nd c.AD)}}; κ. ἂν ἴῃ.., κ. ἄπεισιν {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 370e; κ. τινὰ ἐξαποστεῖλαι LXX.Gen.31.42}}; **bereft of** her mate, λέαινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 986}}; **orphan**, Ἔρωτες {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus 1.59}}; ὑπ᾽ ἄσθματος κενοί **exhausted**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 484}}; of places, **without garrison**, χῶραι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.146, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.15}}; of the body, **without flesh**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.831c.}}
**II.2.b** **devoid of wit, vain, pretentious**, κεινὸς εἴην {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.45; διαπτυχθέντες ὤφθησαν κενοί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 709; ἀνόητον καὶ κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 530, cf. NT.Jam.2.20.}}
**III** _comparative_ and _superlative_, κενότερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 10 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}; -ότατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.25, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.67 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, +others, compare 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.76, “Etymologicum Magnum” 275.50}}; κενώτερος, -ώτατος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 175d, variant in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1107a30 (_comparative_)}}; κενεώτερος Timo (see. above); κενεώτατος variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 62.}}

2762:
** κεραία**,
_poetry_ **κεν-αίη**, ἡ, (κέρας) **horn**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 36, 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 3.476.}}
**2** **antennae** of the crayfish or of insects, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 526a6. 532a26.}}
**II** **anything projecting like a horn**: hence,
**II.1** **yard-arm**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 557 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.41, “IG” 22.657, 1604.17, “PMagd.” 11.4 (3rd c.BC)}}, etc.; κ. καθελέσθαι, ὑφιέναι, i.e.lower sail, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 14.10.11, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.169b; opposed to ἐντείνασθαι Callias Epigrammaticus “anonymous fragment” 382; ἀπὸ ψιλῆς τῆς κ. 'under bare poles', 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 19.}}
**II.1.b** **projecting beam** of a crane, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.76, compare 4.100, “IG” 11(2).161 A 90 (Delos, 3rd c.BC), 3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 100.18, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.5.10, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.19.2.}}
**II.1.c** **projecting parts** of the hucklebone, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 499b30.}}
**II.1.d** **branching stake of wood**, used as a **pale** in a palisade, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.18.7, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 4.78.}}
**II.1.e** **horns of the ancilia**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 13.}}
**II.2** _plural_, **horns** of the moon, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 785, 790.}}
**II.3** in writing, **apex** of a letter, {{NT+4th c.BC+|“IG” 2.4321.10 (4th c.BC), 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 28.27, cf. NT.Matt.5.18, NT.Luke.16.17, 2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 45.57.4; ζυγομαχεῖν περὶ συλλαβῶν καὶ κ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1100a}}; διὰ πάσης κ. διῆκον showing itself in every **word** of a speech, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Dinarcho” 7.}}
**II.4** **leg** of a pair of compasses, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 10.54.}}
**II.5** **projecting spur** of a mountain, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 13}}; of the **horns** of Europe and Africa at the Straits of Gibraltar, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 4.3b.40 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus)}}; **arms** of a harbour, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.21.2.}}
**II.6** ={κέρας} see {{Refs|3}}, **wing** of an army, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 9.20.}}
**II.7** _plural_, supposed teat-like **projections** inside the womb, {{Refs|Diocl.fragment 27}}; but the **Fallopian tubes**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 14.11, 2nd c.AD: Rufus Medicus “περὶ ὀνομασίας” 194.}}
**III** **bow made of horn,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 6.75 (6th c.AD: Paulus Silentiarius Epigrammaticus).}}

2763:
** κερᾰμ-εύς**, έως, ὁ,
**potter**, ὡς ὅτε τις τροχὸν.. κεραμεὺς πειρήσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.601, compare 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 14.1, etc.}}; οἱ κ., a guild at Thyatira, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IGRom.” 4.1205: proverbial, καὶ κεραμεὺς κεραμεῖ κοτέει 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 25, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1381b16, “EN” 1155a35}}; κεραμέως πλοῦτος and κεραμεὺς ἅνθρωπος, proverbial, of anything frail and uncertain, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Diogenianus Paroemiographus 5.97, 98.}}
**II** **Κεραμεῖς**, _Attic dialect_ **Κεραμῆς**, οἱ, name of an Attic deme, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1093 (_anapaest meter_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 315d, etc.}}

2765:
** κερᾰμ-ιον**, τό,
**earthenware vessel, jar,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.1672.13, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 88 (_plural_), etc.; κ. οἰνηρόν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.6, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 78; οἴνου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.15; ὀξηρόν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 723; κ. ταριχηρόν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 534a21}}; ταρίχους, as a measure, Test. cited in {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 35.34, compare “PSI” 5.585 (3rd c.BC), “OGI” 90.31 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC), LXX.Isa.5.10, “SIG” 1109.162 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** **sarcophagus**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 42.26.}}

2766:
** κέρᾰμος**, ὁ,
rare _plural_ κέραμα, τά, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3P. 327 (3rd c.BC)}}:— **potter's earth, potter's clay**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 60d, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 384b19, etc.; κ. ὠμός, ὀπτώμενος, [prev. work] 380b8, 383a21.}}
**II** **anything made of this earth**, as
**II.1** **earthen vessel, wine-jar**, ἐκ κεράμων μέθυ πίνετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.469, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.96}}; in collective sense, **pottery**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 902, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 75, +others}}; κ. ἐσάγεται πλήρης οἴνου **jars** full of wine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.6, compare 5.88, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 257.3, etc.}}
**II.1.b** **jar** of other material, κ. ἀργυροῦς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Ptolemaeus Euergetes II Historicus 7J.}}
**II.2** **tile**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1295 (of a tortoise's shell)}}; collectively, **tiling**, τοῦ τέγους τὸν κέραμον αὐτοῦ χαλάζαις.. ξυντρίψομεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Nu.” 1127, compare “fragment” 349, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.4; Κορίνθιος κ. “IG” 22.1668.58; Λακωνικός [prev. work]463.69, 1672.188}} **roof**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 130.6, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.44, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.26, 9.824.}}
**II.3** **pottery** (i.e.place of manufacture), ὁ κ. ὁ χυτρικός {{Refs|“Tab.Defix.Praef.” p.iib.}}
**III** **dungeon** (said by {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia to be Cyprian), χαλκέῳ ἐν κεράμῳ δέδετο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.387, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 6.6 codex M; _plural_, 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 16.162}}. (Possibly _cognate_ with Latin _cremo._)

2767:
** κεράνν-ῡμι**,
also **κεραννύω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Comicus 15, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 69}}; _Epic dialect_ **κεραίω** and **κεράω** (which see); _subjunctive_ κεραννύω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 61b}}: _imperfect_ ἐκεράννυν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.7:}}_future_ κεράσω [ᾰ] {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “Or.” 27 p}}.340D.: _aorist_ ἐκέρᾰσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 3, (ἐν-) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 427c, poetry κέρᾰσα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 127 (Lyric poetry)}}, _Epic dialect_ κέρασσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.93}}, _Ionic dialect_ ἔκρησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 35}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ ἐκερᾰσάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 95e}}, _Epic dialect_ κεράσσατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.423:}} —_passive_, _future_ κραθήσομαι [ᾱ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 326c, (συγ-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 406:}}_aorist_ ἐκράθην [ᾱ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.5, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1016, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 86c}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἐκρήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 19; ἐκεράσθην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 47c, “Ti.” 85a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.4.29, 3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 10J.}}: _perfect_ κέκρᾱμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.41, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ κέκρημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 13, “Acut.” 21; κεκέρασμαι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 549, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 24, “Anacreontea” 16.13, etc.}}: _pluperfect_ ἐκέκρᾱτο {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 51.1}}:—**mix, mingle** (different from μείγνυμι, see at {κρᾶσις}):
**1** mostly of diluting wine with water, κερῶντάς τ᾽ αἴθοπα οἶνον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.364; κέρασσε δὲ νέκταρ ἐρυθρόν 5.93; κέρασον ἄκρατον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 1123, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.32: _absolutely_, τοῖς θεοῖς εὐχόμενοι κεραννύωμεν}} **let us mix a cup of wine**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 61b; ἂν μὴ κεράσῃ τις 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 85.2}}: with _dative_ _person_, **give to drink**, ἐν τῷ ποτηρίῳ ᾧ ἐκέρασε κεράσατε αὐτῇ διπλοῦν {{NT+8th c.BC+|NT.Rev.18.6:— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus mostly in}}_middle_, ὅτε περ.. οἶνον.. ἐνὶ κρητῆρι κέρωνται **mix their** wine in the bowl, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.260, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.253}}; κρητῆρα κεράσσατο he **mixed** a bowl, {{Refs|3.393, 18.423}}:—_passive_, πῶς οὖν κέκραται [σκύφος]; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 557}}; κύλιξ ἴσον ἴσῳ κεκραμένη a cup **mixed** half and half, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1132}}; κεκρ. τρία καὶ δύο {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Eq.” 1187, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 11.137 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus).}}
**2** **temper, cool by mixing**, θυμῆρες κεράσασα **having mixed** (the water) **to an** agreeable **temperature**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.362.}}
**3** generally, **mix, blend**, ἡδονὴν φθόνῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 50a; τοῖς ὀνόμασι τὰ ῥήματα [prev. author] “Sph.” 262c; νοῦς μετ᾽ αἰσθήσεων κραθείς [prev. author] “Lg.” 961d, compare “Ti.” [same place]; πίστεως αἰσθήσει κεκραμένης 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.7.15; ἀγωγὴν ἐξἀμφοῖν κ. 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “Academicorum Index” p.77 M.; [οὐσία] οὐκ ἀπὸ τῶν ἄκρων κραθεῖσα 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 4.139a}}; of metals, ἀργυρίῳ πρὸς χαλκὸν κεκραμένῳ χρῆσθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.214}}: _metaphorically_, **temper, regulate**, of climates, ὧραι κάλλιστα κεκρημέναι most **temperate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.106; ὧραι μετριώτατα κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 111e; ἔαρ κ. τῇ ὥρᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.5; [πλοῦτον] ἀρετᾷ κεκραμένον 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.2}}; οὐ γῆρας κέκραται γενεᾷ no old age **is mingled with** the race, i.e.it knows no old age,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 10.41, compare “O.” 10(11).104; ἐν ταῖς εὖ κεκρ. πολιτείαις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1307b30}}; of tempers of mind, ἤθει γεννικωτέρῳ κεκρᾶσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 279a; τοῖς ἤθεσιν.. τούτοις ἡ φύσις κεράννυται 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 278b (iii p.744 K.)}}; of Music, ἁρμονίας ῥυθμοῖς κραθείσας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 835b; τῆς εὖ κεκραμένης ἁρμονίας 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1290a26; μετρίως κραθῆναι πρὸς ἄλληλα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phd.” [prev. cited]}}
**II** **compound**, ἐκ τῶν ἐπιτηδευμάτων τὸ ἀνδρείκελον {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 501b; οὐκ ἀπίθανον λόγον [prev. author] “Phdr.” 265b}}; θεόσυτος ἢ βρότειος ἢ κεκραμέν; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 116; φωνὴ μεταξὺ τῆς τε Χαλκιδέων καὶ Δωρίδος ἐκράθη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.5.}}
**III** Grammars, in _passive_, **coalesce by crasis**, τὸ ῥῆμα καὶ ὁ σύνδεσμος συναλοιφῇ κερασθέντα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 22.}}
**IV** **multiply into**, ὅταν ὁ τῆς δεκάδος λόγος τῷ τῆς ἑβδομάδος κερασθῇ {{Refs|“Theologumena Arithmeticae” 50.}}

2768:
** κέρᾰς**, τό,
_Epic dialect_ _genitive_ Κέρᾰος, _Attic dialect_ _contraction_ κέρως; _Epic dialect_ _dative_ κέρᾰϊ (elided) or κέραι or κέρᾳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.385, cf. Hdn.Gr. 2.75, κέρᾳ also in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.90, 7.6:}} _nominative_ _plural_ κέρᾱ (see. below), _genitive_ κεράων, κερῶν, _dative_ κέρασι, _Epic dialect_ κεράεσσι:—_Attic dialect_ Inscrr. have dual [κέρ]ατε {{Refs|“IG” 12.301.109: _plural_ κέρατα [prev. work] 237.59}} later _Epic dialect_ κεράατα ([music notation]) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 291, κεράατος ([music notation]) 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 174, 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 6.225; _dative_ κεράασι [musical notation] A.R.4.978::—Hdt.has _genitive_ κέρεος 6.111, _dative_ κέρεϊ 9.102: _plural_ κέρεα 2.38, κερέων [prev. work] 132; but 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus has _genitive_ _sing._ κέρως, _plural_ κέρατα, “Aër.” 18. [In _nominative_ and _accusative_ κέρας, ᾰ always: in the oblative cases ᾰ in _Epic dialect_, as κέρᾰσιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.384 (in _contraction_ _dative_ κέρᾱ, _nominative_ _plural_ κέρᾱ compare (Batrachomyomachia 165)}}, a is shortened before a vowel, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.385, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.211)}}; but ᾱ in Trag.and Comedy texts, κέρᾱτος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 43, κέρᾱτα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 921, κεράτων [ᾱ] probably in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 519 (Lyric poetry), κέρᾱσι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 185. In later _Epic dialect_ the quantity varies.] (κέρας is probably related to κάρα}}; compare κεραός.)
**I** **the horn of an animal**, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} mostly of oxen, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.521, etc.; ταῦροι.. εἰς κέρας θυμούμενοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 743}}; ὀφθαλμοὶ δ᾽ ὡς εἰ κέρα ἕστασαν his eyes stood fixed and stiff like **horns**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.211}}; as a symbol of strength, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|LXX.Psa.17(18).3, 4th-5th c.AD: Diogenianus Paroemiographus 7.89, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 662a1}}; of elephants' **tusks**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 2.13, 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 2.494.}}
**I.2** **antennae** of crustaceans, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 526a31, 590b27}}; of the silkworm's **grub**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 551b10.}}
**II** **horn**, as a material, αἱ μὲν γὰρ [πύλαι] κεράεσσι τετεύχαται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.563}}; **the horn** of animals' hoofs, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Longus Scriptor Eroticus 2.28.}}
**III** **anything made of horn**,
**III.1** **bow**, τόξον ἐνώμα.. πειρώμενος.. μὴ κέρα ἶπες ἔδοιεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.395, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.206, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 38, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.75 (6th c.AD: Paulus Silentiarius Epigrammaticus); for 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.385 see below variant}}
**III.2** of musical instruments, **horn for blowing**, σημῆναι τῷ κέρατι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.4, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 802a17}}; also, **the Phrygian flute**, because it was tipped with horn {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|compare (2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.74), αὐλεῖν τῷ κ. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 12.1; καὶ κέρατι μὲν αὐλεῖν Τυρρηνοὶ νομίζουσι 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.76, compare 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.184a.}}
**III.3** **drinking-horn**, ἐκ τοῦ κέρατος αὖ μοι δὸς πιεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 43, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.2.23, “OGI” 214.43 (Didyma, 3rd c.BC)}}; ἐξ ἀργυρέων κ. πίνειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 166, compare “IG” 12.280.77; ἀργυρηλάτοις κέρασι χρυσᾶ στόμια προσβεβλημένοις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 185; ἐκπιόντι χρύσεον κ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 483}}; for measuring liquids, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 13.435.}}
**III.4** Ἀμαλθείας κ. **cornucopiae**, see at {Ἀμάλθεια}.
**IV** βοὸς κ. probably a **horn guard** or **cover** attached to a fishing-line, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.81, cf. Scholia; ἐς πόντον προΐησι βοὸς κέρας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.253; ψάμμῳ κ. αἰὲν ἐρείδων “Anthologia Graeca” 6.230 (1st c.AD(?): Maecius Epigrammaticus), compare 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus “Lex.” see {κέρᾳ ἀγλαέ}, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.977a}}] (also explained as a **fishing-line of ox-hair** (compare belowsee.l), cited in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.976f, compare 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.31}}; perhaps an artificial **bait**).
**IV.2** **clyster-pipe**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 8.32 7.}}
**IV.3** in _plural_, **horn points** with which the writing-reed was tipped, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.227 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus).}}
**V** of objects shaped like horns,
**V.1** a mode of dressing the hair, κέρᾳ ἀγλαέ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.385}} (unless the meaning be **bow**),[{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|compare 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus (at [prev. work]), Herodorus and Apionap. 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis (at [prev. work]): hence κέρας is explained as ={θρίξ} or κόμη, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus “Lex.”, Hdn.Gr. cited in Eust.[same place], 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.31, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}]; compare iv. l, and see at {κεροπλάστης}.
**V.2** **arm** or **branch** of a river, Ὠκεανοῖο κ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 789; Νείλου 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 201; τὸ Μενδήσιον κέρας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.110; ἐν Ἰνδοῖς ἐν τῷ Κέρατι καλουμένῳ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 835b5, compare “Mu.” 393b5}}; τὸ κ. τὸ Βυζαντίων the 'Golden **Horn'**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 7.6.2, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.43.7, Scholia 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.282}}; Ἑσπέρου K., name of a **bay**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hanno Geographus “Periplus - GGM” 14, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.21.2.}}
**V.3** **wing** of an army, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.26, etc.; or fleet, [prev. author] 6.8, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.90, etc.}}; κ. δεξιόν, λαιόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 399, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 704; τὸ εὐώνυμον κ. ἀναπτύσσειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.10.9.}}
**V.3.b** κατὰ κέρας προσβάλλειν, ἐπιπεσεῖν, to attack in **flank**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.78, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.16, etc.}}; κατὰ κ. προσιέναι, ἕπεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 7.1.8 and 28; κατὰ κ. συμπεσών 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.40.14; πρὸς κ. μάχεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.22.}}
**V.3.c** ἐπὶ κέρας ἀνάγειν τὰς νέας to lead a fleet in **column**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.12, compare 14; κατὰ μίαν ἐπὶ κέρως παραπλεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.90, compare 6.32, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.34, 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 67.4}}; of armies, κατὰ κέρας, opposed to ἐπὶ φάλαγγος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.43, compare “An.” 4.6.6, “HG” 7.4.23; εἰς κ. [prev. author] “Eq.Mag.” 4.3; ἐκ κέρατος εἰς φάλαγγα καταστῆσαι [prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.5.15}}; οὐκ ἐλᾶτε πρὸς τὸ δεξιὸν κ.; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 243.}}
**V.4** **corps of**{{Refs|8192}}**men**, ={διφαλαγγαρχία}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 2.6, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 10.7.}}
**V.4.b** = {μεραρχία}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 2.10.}}
**V.4.c** **contingent of thirty-two chariots**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 8, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 22.2.}}
**V.5** **sailyard**, ={κεραία}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.203 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), “OGI” 674.30 (Egypt, 1st c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 6, “POxy.” 2136.6 (3rd c.AD).}}
**V.6** **mountainpeak**, variant in {{Refs|“Hymni Homerici” 1.8}}; **spur**, τὸ κ. τοῦ ὄρους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.7, compare 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 534}}: in _plural_, **extremities** of the earth, γῆς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 2.18 (_plural_).}}
**V.7** in Anatomy, **extremities** of the uterus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἐπικυήσιος” 1, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.266}}; of the diaphragm, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.57.}}
**V.7.b** ἁπαλὸν κ., = {πόσθη}, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 171, compareNeophro (?) in “PLit.Lond.” 77 “fragment” 2.19, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 278, “Anthologia Graeca” 12.95.6 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus).}}
**V.8** of the πήχεις of the lyre, χρυσόδετον κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 244 (Lyric poetry)}} (rather than the **bridge**, because made of **horn**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aelius Dionysius Grammaticus “fragment” 133, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.62).}}
**VI** κέρατα ποιεῖν τινι to give him **horns**, cuckold him, proverbial in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 2.11; compare κερασφόρος 11.}}
**VII** = {κερατίνης}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 1.2.}}
**VIII** = {σταφυλῖνος ἄγριος}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.52}}; ={οἰνάνθη}, uncertain in Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.120.}}

2769:
** κερᾱτ-ιον**, τό,
_diminutive_ of κέρας, **small horn**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.73, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.977a.}}
**2** of the **antennae** of the κάραβος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 526a7}}; of the hermit-crab,{{Refs|[prev. work] 529a27.}}
**3** in _plural_, **curved ends** of the womb {{Refs|compare (κεραία 11.7), [prev. work] 510b19.}}
**4** musical instrument, perhaps **fife** or **clarionet**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 29.32, probably to be read instead of κεραμείου in 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 26.1.4, compare [prev. work] 1a. 2.}}
**II** **carat**, 1/1728 **of a pound**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.775, 2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 6.37, 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “*Mens.” 60.21, 6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Nou.” 32.1}}; = Latin _siliqua_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“OGI” 521.27 (Abydos, 5th-6th c.AD).}}
**III** ={κερατωνία}, {{Refs|Colum. “Arbor.” 25.}}
**IV** see at {κεράτια}.

2770:
** κερδ-αίνω**,
_future_ - ᾰνῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 876, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 8.20, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ -ανέω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.35, 8.60.γ}}; κερδήσω {{NT+5th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.390 (Menecrates Comicus), NT.Jam.4.13, κερδήσομαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.72}}: _aorist 1_ ἐκέρδᾱνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).27, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.134}} codices, etc.; _Ionic dialect_ -ηνα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 14.6, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.5, also ἐκέρδησα [prev. author] 4.152, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 4.13, etc.}}: _perfect_ κεκέρδαγκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 53.5, κεκέρδᾰκα 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.366 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 3rd c.AD(?): Achilles Tatius Astronomus 5.25, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 81.2, etc., κεκέρδηκα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 56.30 (προς-), 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Bellum Judaicum” 1.20.2}}:— _passive_, _aorist_ _participle_ κερδανθείς {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ οἰκονομίας” p.67 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}: _perfect_ κεκερδημένος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 18.6.5}}: (κέρδος):—**gain, derive profit** or **advantage**, κακὰ κ. **make** unfair **gains**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 352; μέγιστα ἐκ φορτίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.152}}; τί κερδαν; what **shall I gain**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 259}}; κ. τινί **gain by** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 604; σμικρὰ κερδανῶ φυγῇ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1301; κέρδος κ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 889 (Lyric poetry); κ. ἓξ τάλαντα 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator [same place]; τὸν χρόνον κερδαίνει ὃν ἔζη οὐ προσῆκον αὐτῷ 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.84}}; κ. λόγον **win** fame, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).27}}; χρηστὰ κ. ἔπη **receive** fair words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 231}}: with _participle_, **gain by** doing.., εἰ δὲ κερδανῶ λέγων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1051 (probably); πολεμοῦντες οὐ κερδαίνομεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1591, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.93; οὐδὲν ἐκμαθοῦσα κερδανεῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 876}}; Μεγάροισι κερδανέομεν περιεοῦσι we **shall gain** by Megara's preservation, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.60.γ}}; also κ. ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 46:}} —_passive_, τὰ κερδανθέντα Phld. [prev. cited]
**2** _absolutely_, **make profit, gain advantage**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 520; τοῦ κ. ἔχου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 28, compare 354}}; ἐξ ἅπαντος, ἀπὸ παντός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ant.” 312, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.9.4; παρά τινων 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.7; πρὸς σοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 191}}; opposed to τὸ τιμᾶσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.44}}; **traffic, make merchandise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1037.}}
**II** in bad sense, **reap** disadvantage from a thing, διπλᾶ δάκρυα κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 518; κερδᾶναι τὸν πολὺ χείρω βίον ἀντὶ θανάτου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 9.}}
**III** **save** or **spare oneself, avoid**, μεγάλα κακά {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 92.10; ὕβριν NT.Act.27.21; τὸ μὴ μιανθῆναι τὰς χεῖρας 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 2.3.2; ἐνόχλησιν 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.14, compare 4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 2.26, “Anthologia Graeca” 10.59 (6th c.AD: Palladas Epigrammaticus).}}

2771:
** κέρδος**, εος, τό,
**gain, profit**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.140, etc.}}; ἐνόησεν ὅππως κ. ἔῃ how **some advantage** can be gained, what is **best** to be done, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.225; οὔ τοι τόδε κ. ἐγὼν ἔσσεσθαι ὀΐω ἡμῖν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.311, etc.}}; ποιέεσθαί τι ἐν κέρδεϊ, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.δ᾽, 6.13; κ. νομίσαι τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.68}}; ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.33; ἤν τι.. δάσωνται κ. ἡγεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.43; ἐκ πονηροῦ πράγματος κ. λαβεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 697}}; μέγ᾽ ἐστὶ κ., ἢν.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mon.” 359; πρὸς τὸ κ. βλέπειν [prev. work] 364}}; _participle_, πᾶν κ. ἡγοῦ ζημιουμένη φυγῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 454}}; κ. ἐστί μοι, with _infinitive_, τί δῆτ᾽ ἐμοὶ ζῆν κ.; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 747}}; τί κ. ἦν αὐτῷ διαβάλλειν ἐμ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 8.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 607, 610}}: _plural_, **gains, profits**, περιβαλλόμενος ἑωυτῷ κέρδεα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71}}; τὰ δειλὰ (variant{δεινὰ}) κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 326; τὰ κ. μείζω φαίνεσθαι τῶν δεινῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.59; τὰ πονηρὰ κ. 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 270:— κ. (_metaphorically_) opposed to ζημία (damage), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1132a12, (literal) opposed to ζημἱα (damages), [prev. work] 14; ζημίαν λαβεῖν ἄμεινόν ἐστιν ἢ κ. κακόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 807.}}
**2** **desire of gain**, κέρδει καὶ σοφία δέδεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.54; ἄνδρας τὸ κ. πολλάκις διώλεσεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 222; εἰς τὸ κ. λῆμ᾽ ἔχων ἀνειμένον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 3: _plural_, κερδῶν ἄθικτος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 704; ἐν τοῖς κέρδεσιν μόνον δέδορκε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 388; μὴ 'πὶ κέρδεσιν λέγων [prev. author] “Ant.” 1061, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1207}}; of persons, ἡμέτερα κ. τῶν σοφῶν (={ἡμῶν τῶν σ}.) **you of whom** we wise men **make gain**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1202.}}
**3** iron. {{Refs|compare (κερδαίνω 11), ἀστεῖόν γε κ. ἔλαβεν ὁ κακοδαίμων [prev. work] 1064.}}
**II** in _plural_, **cunning arts, wiles**, ὃς δέ κε κ. εἰδῇ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.322, compare 709, +others}}; κέρδεσιν, οὔ τι τάχει γε παραφθάμενος{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 515; φρένας ἐσθλὰς κέρδεά θ᾽ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.118, compare 88; ἐγὼ δ᾽ ἐν πᾶσι θεοῖσι μήτι τε κλέομαι καὶ κέρδεσιν 13.299; ἐνὶ φρεσὶ κέρδε᾽ ἐνώμας 18.216}}; κακὰ κ. βουλεύουσιν 'they mean **mischief**', {{Refs|23.217. (Cf. OIr.}}**cerd** 'art', 'craft', Welsh **cerdd** 'craft' or 'music'.)

2772:
** κέρμα**, ατος, τό,
(κείρω) **fragment**, κέρματα θηρείων μελέων uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 101.1; τὰ κ. τοῦ ἡνωμένου ἡνωμένα 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 107, compare 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}; but mostly,
**2** **coin**, ἐγκάψας τὸ κ. εἰς τὴν γνάθον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 128.7; μικροῦ πρίασθαι κ. τὴν ἡδονήν 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 67.7, compare 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 5, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 131}}; collectively, **cash**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 30, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.10.14, +others, “Cat.Codex Astr.” 7.244}} especially of copper money, opposed to silver (ἀργύριον), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PGen.” 77.5 (2nd-3rd c.AD)}}: frequently in _plural_, μικρὰ κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1108, compare “Pl.” 379, 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 84.1}}; διδοὺς κέρματα Test. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.107, compare 4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 89a, “UPZ” 81 iv 20, 145 xi 71 (2nd c.BC), 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.2, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.44 (from Cillactor).}}

2774:
** κεφάλαι-ος** [ᾰ], α, ον,
(κεφαλή) **of the head**: _metaphorically_, **principal, chief**, ῥῆμα κ. (with a play on κεφαλίτης λίθος) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 854; τὸ κ. μέρος “PMasp.” 151.16 (6th c.AD)}}: _superlative_ -ότατος variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 494e.}}
**II** mostly _substantive_ κεφάλαιον, τό, ={κεφαλή}, **head, parts about the head**, especially of fish, θύννου κ. τοδί Callias Comedy texts {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3: in _plural_, 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 35, 3rd c.BC: Sotades Lyricus Comedy texts 1.5; also κ. ῥαφανῖδος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 981}}; of an infant, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Leonidas Medicus cited in 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 6.1.}}
**II.2** **chief** or **main point**, κ. δὴ παιδείας λέγομεν τὴν ὀρθὴν τροφήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 643c}}; especially in speaking or writing, **sum, gist of the matter**, κεφάλαια λόγων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.116; κ. τοῦ παντὸς λόγου 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Γεωργός” 75, compare 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 5.18.1}}; τὰ κ. συγγράφων Εὐριπίδῃ drawing up the **heads** of the play, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 113.5: frequently in Prose, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.50, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 453a, etc.; κ. τῶν εἰρημένων 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.62, compare 5.154; κ. τῆς οἰκονομίας 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.68 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus (_plural_)}}; ἐν κεφαλαίῳ, or ὡς ἐν κ., εἰπεῖν to speak **summarily**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.3.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 186c, +others}}; ἐν κεφαλαίοις ὑπομνῆσαι, ἀποδείξειν, περιλαβεῖν τι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.87, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.33, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.9; βραχυτάτῳ κ. μαθεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.36}}; τύπῳ καὶ ἐπὶ κεφαλαίου (variant -αίῳ), opposed to ἀκριβέστερον, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1107b14; ἐπὶ κ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.65.5, 3.5.9; ἐπὶ κεφαλαίων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.315, etc.}}; especially in an argument, **summing up**, ἐν κεφαλαίοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 26c}}; κεφαλαίῳ δέ.., Latin _denique_, Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.164; τὸ δ᾽ οὖν κ. [prev. work]213; τὸ δὲ κ. τῶν λόγων, ἄνθρωπος εἶ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 531.10}}; συναγαγεῖν τὸ κ. **to sum** up, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1042a4.}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, of persons, **the head** or **chief**, ὅ τι περ κ. τῶν κάτωθεν, of Pericles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 93; τὸ κ. οὐδέπω λογίζομαι, τὸν δεσπότην 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 173; ὅ τι περ τὸ κ. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Harm.” 3, “Gall.” 24, “Philops.” 6}}; τὰ κ. τῶν μαθημάτων, of philosophers, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Pisc.” 14; τὸ κ. τοῦ πολέμου 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 5.50}}; οἳ τὸ τῆς στάσεως κ. ἦσαν{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 43; τὸν Θαλῆν τῶν σοφῶν τὸ κ. 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 3.125d: hence, of qualities, etc., σχεδόν τι τὸ κ. τῶν κακῶν (i.e. avarice) 2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus Gel. 4; τὸ κ. τῆς εὐδαιμονίας ἡ διάθεσις 2nd c.AD: Diogenes Oenoandensis Epicureus 57.}}
**II.4** Rhet., **head, topic** of argument, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 1, “Rh.” 10.5, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 1.2.31.}}
**II.5** of money, **capital**, opposed to interest or income, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 742c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.64, etc.}}
**II.5.b** **sum total,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.91.23, +others, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.40, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.10}}; πολλοῦ κ. for a large _sum, _{{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Acts.22.28, cf. Aristeas 24, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 4, etc.; κ. ἀργυρικά “PRyl.” 133.15 (1st c.AD)}}; also σιτικὰ καὶ ἀργυρικὰ κ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PSI” 4.281.31 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II.6** **crown, completion** of a thing, τὸ μὲν κ. τῶν ἀδικημάτων the **crowning act** of wrong, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.7; δύο ταῦτα ὡσπερεὶ κ. ἐφ᾽ ἅπασι.. ἐπέθηκε [prev. author] 21.18.}}
**II.7** **chapter, section, PGnom.Prooem.**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Ammonius Philosophus “in Aristotelis de Interpretatione commentarius - pars” 1.17, +others, Chor.in “Hermes” 17.223}}; distinguished from τίτλος, {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus s.Hymns of see.}}

2775:
** κεφαλαι-όω**,
**bring under heads, sum up**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.67, +others}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Magna Moralia” 1207b22}}; κ. τινά **characterize generally**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 576b; τὰς δυνάμεις τινῶν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ κακιῶν ί” p.17 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}:—_passive_, **to be summed up**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1013b30; κ. ἑκάστην τῶν ἀρετῶν περὶ ἴδιόν τι κεφάλαιον “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.73}}; κεφαλαιοῦσθαι ἐννακισχιλίων ἑξακοσίων [σταδίων] **to amount in all to**.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.1.39}}; εἰς δύο ἀρτηρίας ἡ πάντων ἀγγείων κ. σύνοδος **is combined in**.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 4.657, compare 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 44}}; κεφαλαιούσθω διότι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.35 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**II** **smite on the head,** {{NT|NT.Mark.12.4.}}

2776:
** κεφᾰλή**, ἡ,
**head** of man or beast, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see.below), 7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 15, etc.; once in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus, “Th.” 525 (Lyric poetry), once in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, “Aj.” 238 (_anapaest meter_), also in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus, “fragment” 308 (_anapaest meter_), “Rh.” 226 (Lyric poetry), +others; ἄλλου οὐδενὸς ἐμψύχου κ. γεύσεται Αἰγυπτίων οὐδείς 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.39}}; κεφαλῇ.. μείζονες taller **in stature**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.168}}; so μείων.. κεφαλήν{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 193 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus: frequently with Preps.}}
**a** κατὰ κεφαλῆς, _Epic dialect_ κὰκ κεφαλῆς, **down over the head**, κόνιν.. χεύατο κὰκ κεφαλῆς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.24, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.85, etc.}}
**b** κατὰ κεφαλήν, _Epic dialect_ κὰκ κεφαλήν **on the head**, Ἐρύλαον.. βάλε πέτρῳ μέσσην κὰκ κεφαλήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.412, compare 20.387, 475}}: in Prose, **from above**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.8}}: c._genitive_, **above**, κ. κ. τινῶν γενέσθαι {{Refs|[prev. work] 7.2.11}}; τὸ κ. κ. ὕδωρ, of rain water, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.10.7 (-ὴν codices), “CP” 6.18.10}} (-ῆς): in Architecture texts, **upright,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.463.42}}; also, **per head, each person** (compare below 1.2), {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1272a14, LXX.Exo.16.16; κατὰ κεφαλὴν τῶν κωμητῶν “PPetr.” 2p.17 (3rd c.BC).}}
**c** ἐς πόδας ἐκ κεφαλῆς from **head** to foot, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.169; τὰ πράγματα ἐκ τῶν ποδῶν ἐς τὴν κ. σοι πάντ᾽ ἐρῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 650.}}
**d** ἐπὶ κεφαλήν **head foremost**, ἐπὶ κ. κατορύξαι to bury **head downwards**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.35}}; ἐπὶ κ. ὠθέεσθαι to be thrust **headlong**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.136, compare 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 251; ἐπὶ κ. ὠθεῖν τινα ἐκ τοῦ θρόνου 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 553b; ἐπὶ τὴν κ. εἰς κόρακας ὦσον 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Σαμία” 138; εὐθὺς ἐπὶ κ. εἰς τὸ δικαστήριον βαδίζειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.12}}; οὐ βουλόμενος πολίτας ἄνδρας ἐπὶ κ. εἰσπράττειν τὸν μισθόν **recklessly**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Lycophrone” 17}}; ἐπὶ ταῖς κεφαλαῖς περιφέρειν carry **on high**, in token of admiration, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 600d.}}
**2** as the noblest part, _periphrastic_ for the whole person, πολλὰς ἰφθίμους κ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.55, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.343, etc.}}; ἶσον ἐμῇ κ. no less than my**self**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.82; ἑᾷ κ. 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.67; especially in salutation, φίλη κ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.281, compare 18.114; ἠθείη κ. 23.94; Ἄπολλον, ὦ δία κ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 226 (Lyric poetry): in Prose, Φαῖδρε, φίλη κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 264a; τῆς θείας κ. 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.212a}}: in bad sense, ὦ κακαὶ κεφαλαί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.29; ὦ μιαρὰ κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 285: periphrastic in Prose, πεντακοσίας κεφαλὰς τῶν Ξέρξεω πολεμίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.99}}: in bad sense, ἡ μιαρὰ καὶ ἀναιδὴς αὕτη κ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.117, compare 18.153; ἡ κ. τῶν αὐτοῦ “PRein.” 57.8 (4th c.AD)}}; μεγάλη κ. a great **personage**, Vett. Val.{{Refs|74.7; compare above 1 b near the end}}
**3** **life**, ἐμῇ κ. περιδείδια {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.242; σύν τε μεγάλῳ ἀπέτεισαν, σὺν σφῇσιν κεφαλῇσι 4.162}}; παρθέμενοι κεφαλάς staking **their heads** on the cast, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.237}}; τὴν κ. ἀποβαλέεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.65.}}
**4** in imprecations, ἐς κεφαλὴν τράποιτ᾽ ἐμοί on my **head** be it! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 833; ἐς τὴν κ. ἅπαντα τὴν σὴν τρέψεται [prev. author] “Nu.” 40; ἃ σοὶ καὶ τοῖς σοῖς οἱ θεοὶ τρέψειαν εἰς κ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.290}}; ἐς κ. σοί (i.e. τράποιτο) {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1063, “Pl.” 526; σοὶ εἰς κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 283e; τὰ μὲν πρότερον.. ἐγὼ κεφαλῇ ἀναμάξας φέρω 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.155; οἷς ἂν.. τὴν αἰτίαν ἐπὶ τὴν κ. ἀναθεῖεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.294; τὸ αἷμα ὑμῶν ἐπὶ τὴν κ. ὑμῶν NT.Act.18.6.}}
**II** of things, **extremity**,
**II.a** in Botany, κ. σκορόδου **head** (= **inflorescence**) of garlic, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 718, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.6.4; κ. μήκωνος 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.8.2}}; ῥίζα κ. ἔχουσα πλείονας **tubers**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.120.}}
**II.b** in Anatomy, κεφαλαὶ τῆς κάτω γνάθου, probably the condyloid and coronoid processes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 30}}; ἡ κ. τοῦ ὄρχεως, ={ἐπιδιδυμίς}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 510a14, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 4.565}}; μηροῦ, κνήμης κ., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.186, 188}}; of the **base** of the heart, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 6.16}}; but, **apex**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ καρδίης” 7}}; of the **sac** in poulps, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 654a23, 685a5}}; of muscles, **origin**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 7.14.}}
**II.c** generally, **top, brim** of a vessel, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 8.87}}; **coping** of a wall, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.68}}; **capital** of a column, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2782.31 (from Aphrodisias), LXX.3Ki.7.16, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.121.}}
**II.d** in _plural_, **source** of a river, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.91}} (butsg., **mouth**, οἶδα Γέλα ποταμοῦ κεφαλῇ ἐπικείμενον ἄστυ {{Refs|Callias Epigrammaticus “Aet.Oxy.” 2080.48)}}: generally, **source, origin**, Ζεὺς κ. (variant{ἀρχή}), Ζεὺς μέσσα, Διὸς δ᾽ ἒκ πάντα τελεῖται τέτυκται codices) {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 21a}}; **starting-point**, κ. χρόνου {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Placita Philosophorum” 2.32.2 (κρόνου codices), 6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “de Mensibus” 3.4; κ. μηνός [prev. work] 12.}}
**II.e** **extremity** of a plot of land, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.72 (3rd c.BC), “PFlor.” 50.83 (3rd c.AD).}}
**III** Ὁμηρείη κ. **bust** of Homer, {{Refs|“IG” 14.1183.10.}}
**IV** κ. περίθετος **wig, head-dress**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 258.}}
**V** _metaphorically_, κ. δείπνου **pièce de résistance**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 172.15.}}
**V.2** **crown, completion**, κεφαλὴν ἐπιθεῖναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 69b; ὥσπερ κ. ἀποδοῦναι τοῖς εἰρημένοις [prev. author] “Phlb.” 66d, compare “Grg.” 505d; ὥσπερ κεφαλὴν ἔχουσα ἐπιστήμη 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1141a19}}; **consummation**, σχεῖν κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 39d.}}
**V.3** **sum, total**, πάσας ἐρρηγείας {{Refs|“Tab.Heracl.” 1.36}}; of money, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 12(9).7 (Carystus, 4th c.BC), “SIG” 245ii 36 (Delph., 4th c.BC).}}
**V.4** **band** of men, {{LXX|LXX.Job.1.17}}; **right-hand half** of a phalanx (opposed to οὐρά), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 8.3, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 7.3.}}
**V.5** Astron., κ. τοῦ κόσμου, of Aries, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 1.1}}. (**ghebh**-, compare κεβλή and Engl. **gable.**)

2777:
** κεφᾰλ-ίς**, ίδος, ἡ,
_diminutive_ of κεφαλή, **little head**, σκορόδου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMeretr.” 14.3}}; **head** of a nail, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 11.488c}}; **extremity**, τῶν σκυταλίδων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 44.23.74.}}
**II** **capital** of a column, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.147, Chor. p.118 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus (_plural_), “PLond.” 3.755v6 (4th c.AD), “Geoponica” 14.6.6 (_plural_)}}: _plural_, ={κρόσσαι}, {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 903.6.}}
**III** **toe-cap** of a shoe, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1392a31}}, cf. Anon. (at [prev. work]); of the **foot** of a table, {{Refs|Aristeas 68.}}
**IV** **rope attached to the bow** of a ship, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 3.9.38 (_plural_).}}
**V** κ. βιβλίου **roll**, {{LXX|LXX.Eze.2.9, LXX.Psa.38(39).8, +others}}

2778:
** κῆνσος**, ὁ,
= Latin _census,_ {{NT+3rd c.AD+|NT.Matt.22.19, “IGRom.” 4.1213 (from Thyatira), 3.41 (from Nicaea), “PAmh.” 2.83.2 (3rd-4th c.AD).}}
**II** **poll-tax, **{{NT|NT.Matt.17.25.}}

2779:
** κῆπος**,
_Doric dialect_**κᾶπος** (also {{Refs|“Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.20 H.)}}, ὁ, **garden, orchard**, or **plantation**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.129, 24.247, 338; πολυδένδρεος 4.737}}; of any rich, highly cultivated region, as Ἀφροδίτας κᾶπος, i.e. Cyrene, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.24; Διὸς κ., i.e. Libya, [prev. work] 9.53 (but Διὸς κῆποι}}, also of heaven, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 320 (Lyric poetry)}}; Φοίβου παλαιὸς κ., of the eastern sky,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 956, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 203b; compare Ὠκεανοῦ κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 271); κ. Εὐβοίας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 24}}; οἱ Μίδεω κῆποι, in Macedonia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.138}}; of the country round Panormus, {{Refs|Callias Epigrammaticus Hist. 2}}; the **enclosure** for the Olympic games, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.24}}; οἱ ἀπὸ τῶν κ. the scholars of Epicurus, because he taught in a **garden**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 9.64, compare 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 10.10}}; οἱ Ἀδώνιδος κ., see at {Ἀδωνι; οἱ Ταντάλου κ}., proverbial of illusory pleasures, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.20.1: _metaphorically_, Χαρίτων νέμομαι κᾶπον, i.e. poetic art, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.27; ἐκ Μουσῶν κ. τινῶν.. δρεπόμενοι τὰ μέλη 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 534a; τοὺς ἐν τοῖς γράμμασι κ. σπείρειν [prev. author] “Phdr.” 276d.}}
**II** **a fashion of cropping the hair**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 2.29, 2nd c.AD: Aelius Dionysius Grammaticus “fragment” 230.}}
**III** **pudenda muliebria**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.116.}}
**IV** variant for {κῆβος} (which see).

2780:
** κηπουρ-ός**, ὁ, οὖρος
) **keeper of a garden**, ὄφις {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euphorio Epicus 154.}}
**II** **gardener,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.10 (5th c.BC), 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.2.5, “PCair.Zen.” 59.6 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.6.4, NT.John.20.15, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Her.Prooem.” 2, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4082 (from Pessinus)}}; title of play by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus}}:—also **κηπωρός**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus 44, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Minos” 316e, “PFay.” 101r ii4 (1st c.BC).}}

2781:
** κηρίον**, τό, κηρός
) **honeycomb**, mostly in _plural_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 559, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 597, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.114, etc.}}; κ. καὶ λίβανον, as offerings, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Supp.epigram” 3.774 (Crete, 1st c.BC): _sing._, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 552c, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 19.2, “IG” 5(2).514.14 (Lycosura, 2nd c.BC)}}; τὸ κ. τοῦ μέλιτος {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.14.27}}; used in Medicine, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 2.45, 3.17}}; παιδίον κηρίῳ βεβυσμένον having its mouth stopped with **a piece of honeycomb**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 506, cf. Scholia (at [prev. work]), 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.86; κ. σφηκῶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.92: κηρία, τά}}, **honey**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 36, 1st c.BC(?): Aristo Epigrammaticus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.38f.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, of a book of poems, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.190: _plural_, title of Anthologies, 2nd c.AD: Gellius, Aulus Grammaticus Latinus “Praef.” 6.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, of anything pleasant, τῆς ἐπιθυμίας τὸ κ. {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 112.1.}}
**II** **a cutaneous disease**, ={μελικηρίς}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.135 (_plural_), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 7.728, +others}}

2782:
** κήρυγ-μα**, ατος, τό,
(κηρύσσω) **that which is cried by a herald, proclamation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 13, etc.; κ. ποιέεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.52}}; ἐκ τοῦ κ. by **proclamation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.78; πόλει κ. θεῖναι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 8; τῷ κ. ἐμμένειν [prev. author] “OT” 350, compare “Ant.” 454; κ. ἀνειπεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.105; κηρύσσειν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.154; κ. γιγνόμενον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.83}}; **announcement** of victory in games, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 63.14}}; **mandate, summons**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 162 (_anapaest meter_)}}; **reward offered by proclamation**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.10, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.33.}}
**II** **preaching,** {{NT|NT.Luke.11.32, +others}}

2783:
** κῆρυξ**, ῡκος, ὁ,
_Aeolic dialect_ **κᾶρυξ** [ᾱ] {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 20a. 2, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.1}}:—but **κήρῡκος**, ου, ὁ, {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 775.26}}: (κηρύσσω):— **herald, pursuivant**: generally, **public messenger, envoy**, κ. λιγύφθογγοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.50, +others; κηρύκων, οἳ δημιοεργοὶ ἔασιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.135; κ. Διῒφίλοι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.517; κ., Διὸς ἄγγελοι ἠδὲ καὶ ἀνδρῶν 1.334}}; θεῶν κ., of Hermes, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 80, compare “Th.” 939, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 515, “Ch.” 124: distinguished from πρέσβεις}}, as being **messengers between nations at war**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.29, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 727, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 941a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 12.4: used interchangeably with ἀπόστολος, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.21}}: as _proper name_ of a family at Athens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.53, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.116, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.38.3, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.103; functioning as μάγειροι at festivals, Clidem. 3, 17; Κηρυκίδαι 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}
**b** as _feminine_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.1, 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 4.11.}}
**2** **crier**, who made proclamation and kept order in assemblies, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 42f; ὁ κ. ἀνεῖπεν 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.36, etc.}}; ὁ τῶν μυστῶν κ., at Eleusis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.20, compare “SIG” 845 (Eleusis, 3rd c.AD), 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.33.4.}}
**3** **auctioneer**, ὑπὸ κήρυκος πωλεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 97; ἀπέδοτο πάντα τὰ ἔργα ὑπὸ κήρυκα “IPE” 12.32 B 35 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC),compare “PHib.” 1.29.21 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἀποδίδοσθαι ὑπὸ κήρυκι [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Ammonius Grammaticus “περὶ ὁμοίων καὶ διαφόρων λέξεων” p.81 V. (variant{ὑπὸ κήρυκα} Ptol.Asc. p.399 H.).}}]
**4** generally, **messenger, herald**, θεοὶ κήρυκες ἀγγέλλουσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1511, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 347}}; of the cock, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 30}}; of writing, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Th.” 780 (_anapaest meter_); κ. καὶ τάφος εἰμὶ βροτοῦ “IG” 14.1618}}; of Homer, ἡρώων κάρυκ᾽ ἀρετᾶς {{NT|[prev. work]1188: _metaphorically_, κ. καὶ ἀπόστολος NT.1Tim.2.7, +others}}
**II** **trumpet-shell**, e.g. **Triton nodiferum**, and smaller species, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 528a10, +others, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.48, Diocl.fragment 133, 3rd c.BC: Macho Comicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.349c, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 4.670, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.7, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor Trall. 3.7}}. [ῡ except _accusative_ _plural_ κήρῠκας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Antimachus Colophonius Elegiacus et Epicus 19 (assuming variant), compare κηρῠκιον “Anthologia Graeca” 11.124 (1st c.AD: Nicarchus Epigrammaticus): but accented κῆρυξ, Hdn.Gr. 1.44}}, etc.] (Cf. Sanskrit _kārús_ 'poet', **kīrtis** 'fame'.)

2784:
** κηρύσσω**,
_Attic dialect_ **κηρύττω**, _Doric dialect_ **κᾱρύσσω**: _imperfect_ ἐκήρυσσον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.444, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.27, -υττον 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.112}}: _future_ -ύξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 684}}, _Doric dialect_ καρυξῶ {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ach.” 748}}: _aorist_ ἐκήρυξα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.194 (ἀπο-)}}, etc., _Aeolic dialect_ _participle_ καρύξαισα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4 (3). 25:}}_perfect_ κεκήρῡχα (ἐπι-) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.35}}:—_passive_, _future_ κηρυχθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.4, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.230, κηρύξομαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1631}}: _aorist_ ἐκηρύχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 737, etc.}}: _perfect_ κεκήρυγμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.38}}:—**to be a herald, officiate as herald**, κηρύσσων γήρασκε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.325.}}
**b** **to be an auctioneer**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.4.}}
**2** **make proclamation as a herald**, λαὸν κηρύσσοντες ἀγειρόντων let them convene the people **by voice of herald**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.438, compare 444, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.8; κήρυσσε, κῆρυξ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 566, etc.: _impersonal_, ἐκήρυξε (i.e. ὁ κῆρυξ) τοῖς Ἕλλησι συσκευάζεσθαι}} **proclamation was made**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.36; κηρυξάτω [prev. author] “Cyr.” 4.5.42.}}
**II** with _accusative_ _person_, **summon by herald**, κ. ἀγορήνδε.. Ἀχαιούς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.51, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.7; πόλεμόνδε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.443}}; κ. τινά **summon** one to a place, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 748:}} —_passive_, τίς ἐκηρύχθη πρώτην φυλακή; who **was summoned** to the first watch? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 538 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II.2** **proclaim** as conqueror, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.185a; Φαβωρῖνον ἡ εὐγλωττία ἐν σοφισταῖς ἐκήρυττεν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 1.8.1}}:—_passive_, μήτε κηρυχθήσεσθαι μήτε ἆθλα λήψεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.4; ὥστε τὴν πόλιν κηρυχθῆναι καὶ αὐτὸν στεφανωθῆναι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.63}}; **proclaim** as a criminal, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.56, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 606; κηρύσσω τὸν Ἔρωτα “Anthologia Graeca” 5.176 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus)}}:—_passive_, of a country, **to be proclaimed, extolled**, στεφάνοις ἀρετᾶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 223 (Lyric poetry).}}
**II.3** **call upon, invoke**, θεούς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hec.” 146 (_anapaest meter_); κηρύξας δαίμονας κλύειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 124a}}:—_passive_, **to be called**, τοῦ κεκήρυξαι πατρό; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1; κηρυσσομένοισι.. ἀπ᾽ ἐσθλῶν δωμάτων [prev. author] “Andr.” 772 (Lyric poetry).}}
**III** with _accusative_ _of things_, **proclaim, announce**, τινί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1349, “Ch.” 4, 1026; αὐδάν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 911 (Lyric poetry); ἀγῶνας Ἀργείοισι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1240}}; **proclaim** or **advertise** for sale, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.121 (_passive_), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.207a, etc.}}; κ. ἀποικίαν **proclaim** a colony, i.e. **invite** people to join as colonists, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.27}}; κ. ὃς βούλοιτο.. **make proclamation** for some one who would.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.134}}:— _passive_, of a crime, **to be proclaimed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.2; τὰ κεκηρυγμένα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.38.}}
**III.2** **declare, tell**, τοῦτ᾽ ἐκηρύχθη πόλει this **news was spread** in.., {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “OT” [same place]; τοῦτο κ. πόθι παῖς ναίει [prev. author] “Tr.” 97 (Lyric poetry); ὃ εἰς τὸ οὖς ἀκούετε, κηρύξατε ἐπὶ τῶν δωμάτων NT.Matt.10.27: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1105.}}
**III.3** **proclaim, command publicly**, τινί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1048, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 32, 450, etc.}}; εὐφημίαν, σιγὴν κ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “fragment” 893, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 530}}: with _dative_ _person_ et _infinitive_, κ. αὐτοῖς ἐμβαλεῖν κώπαισι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.200; ἐκήρυξαν, εἰ βούλονται, τὰ ὅπλα παραδοῦναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.37:}} —_passive_, ᾔδησθα κηρυχθέντα μὴ πράσσειν τάδε{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 447.}}
**III.4** of a cock, **crow,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca 5.2 (Antip. Thess.).}}
**IV** **preach, teach publicly,** {{NT|NT.Matt.3.1, +others}}

2785:
** κῆτος**, εος, τό,
**any sea-monster** or **huge fish**, δελφῖνάς τε κύνας τε καὶ εἴ ποθι μεῖζον ἑλῃσι κῆτος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.97, compare 5.421, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.147, 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 2.116}}; of seals, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.446, 452}}; of the monster to which Andromeda was exposed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 121, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 556, “Th.” 1033}}; of the tunny, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Archestratus Epicus “fragment” 34.3.}}
**2** in Natural History, of the spouting **cetacea**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 566b2, “PA” 669a8, 697a16.}}
**II** name of a constellation, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 354, 4th c.BC: Eudoxus Astronomus cited in 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.2.20.}}

2788:
** κῐθάρ-α**,
_Ionic dialect_ **κῐθάρ-η** [θᾰ], ἡ, **lyre**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.24, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 79, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 882 (_anapaest meter_)}}, etc.; compare κίθαρις.
**II** ={κίθαρος}, **thorax,** {{Refs|Hippiatr. 46}}: in _plural_, **ribs** of the horse, {{Refs|[prev. work] 38.}}

2790:
** κῐθᾰρῳδ-ός**, ὁ,
(κιθάρα, ἀοιδός) **one who plays and sings to the cithara**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.23, “IG” 12.547, 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 6.1, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.28K., etc.: as _feminine_, κ. γυνή 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.33.}}
**II** **a fish**, found in the Red Sea, with body striped like the strings of a lyre, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 11.23.}}

2792:
** κιννᾰμωμ-ον**, τό,
= Hebrew **[kudot]innamon**, a superior kind of **cassia, Cinnamomum Cassia**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.111, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.5.1, “PSI” 6.628 (3rd c.BC), “OGI” 214.59 (Didyma, 3rd c.BC)}}, etc.: **κῐνάμωμον**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 945 (_plural_)}}, also in codices of {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.91}}, variant in Hdt.[same place]; compare κίνναμον.
**II** name of a fabulous Indian bird, said to make its nest of twigs of κιννάμωμον (compare κινναμολόγος), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 616a6, 3rd c.BC: Antigonus Carystius Paradoxographus “Mirabilia” 43, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 2.34.}}

2793:
** κινδυν-εύω**,
_future_ -σω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.60}}.α᾽, etc.: _perfect_ κεκινδύνευκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.47, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.61.4}}:—_passive_, mostly in _present_: _future_ κινδυνευθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.10, κεκινδυνεύσομαι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.75}}: _aorist_ and _perfect_, see below 3: (κίνδυνος):—**to be daring, run risk**, κ. πρὸς πολλούς, πρὸς τοὺς πολεμίους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.3.14}}; κ. εἰς τὴν Αῐγυπτον **venture** thither, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 11.}}
**b** _absolutely_, **make a venture, take a risk**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.69, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1204}}; **to be in dire peril**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.28, 6.33, etc.}}; **to be in danger**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1124b8, etc.}}; of a sick person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Aph. (Sp.”) 7.82, “Coac.” 374}}; especially **engage in war**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.43}}; τοῦ χωρίου κινδυνεύοντος the post **being in peril**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.8}}; ὁ κινδυνεύων τόπος the place **of danger**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.115.6.}}
**2** with _dative_, κ. τῷ σώματι, τῇ ψυχῇ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.120, 7.209}}; κ. ἁπάσῃ τῇ Ἑλλάδι **run a risk with** all Greece, i.e. endanger it all, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.60. α᾽; στρατιῇ [prev. author] 4.80}}; τίσιν οὖν ὑμεῖς κινδυνεύσαιτ᾽ ἄν..; **in** what points.. ? {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.18}}; κ. τοῖς ὅλοις πράγμασι, τῷ βίῳ, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.70.1, 5.61.4; τῷ ζῆν “PTeb.” 44.21 (2nd c.BC)}}: frequently with Preps., κ. ἐν τοῖς σώμασι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.63; οὐκ ἐν τῷ Καρὶ ἀλλ᾽ ἐν υἱέσι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 187b (_passive_)}}; κ. περὶ [τῆς Πελοποννήσου] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.74; περὶ τῆς ψυχῆς 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 524; περὶ τοῦ σώματος 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.4; περὶ ἀνδραποδισμοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.37; περὶ τῆς μεγίστης ζημίας 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.15; περὶ τῆς βασιλείας πρὸς Κῦρον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 15.24; περὶ αὑτῷ 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.6; περὶ τοῖς φιλτάτοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314a}}; but κ. περὶ δισχιλίους **go into battle** with a force of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2, 000, 4th-5th c.AD: Eunapius Historicus “Fragmenta Historica - HGM” p.244 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; ὑπὲρ καλλίστων 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.79.}}
**3** with _accusative_ _cognate_, **venture, hazard**, τοὺς ἐσχάτους κινδύνους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.82; κινδύνευμα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 451a; μάχην 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2.169}}; τὴν ψευδομαρτυρίαν **hazard** a prosecution for perjury, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 41.16 codices (τῶν-ιῶν Blass)}}:—_passive_, **to be ventured** or **hazarded**, μεταβολὴ κινδυνεύεται **there is risk** of change, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.43}}; ὁποτέρως ἔσται, ἐν ἀδήλῳ κινδυνεύεται **remains in hazardous** uncertainty, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.78; τὰ μέγιστα κινδυνεύεται τῇ πόλει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.285}}; κεκινδυνευμένον **a venturous enterprise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.14}}; τὰ κινδυνευθέντα, ={τὰ κινδυνεύματα}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.54; τῶν ἤδη σφίσι καλῶς κεκινδυνευμένων 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 2.7.3; τὸ φιλοπόλεμον καὶ κεκ. 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.21.}}
**4** with _infinitive_, **run the risk** of doing or being.., τὸν στρατὸν κινδυνεύσει ἀποβαλεῖν {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.65; κακόν τι λαβεῖν [prev. author] 6.9; ἀπολέσθαι [prev. author] 9.89; διαφθαρῆναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.74; ἀποθανεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28b, etc.; τοῦ συντριβῆναι LXX.Jonah.1.4}}; then,
**4.b** to express **chance**, i.e. what **may possibly** or **probably happen**: with _present_, _perfect_, or _aorist_ _infinitive_, κινδυνεύουσι οἱ ἄνθρωποι οὗτοι γόητες εἶναι they **run a risk of being reputed** conjurers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.105}}; κινδυνεύσομεν βοηθεῖν we **shall probably have** to assist, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 164e, compare 172c}}; κ. ἡ ἀληθὴς δόξα ἐπιστήμη εἶναι **seems likely** to be.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 187b}}; κινδυνεύσεις ἐπιδεῖξαι χρηστὸς εἶναι you **will have the chance of** showing your worth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.3.17, compare 3.13.3}}; κ. ἀναμφιλογώτατον ἀγαθὸν εἶναι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 4.2.34, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 40b}}; τὰ συσσίτια κινδυνεύει συναγαγεῖν he **probably** organized the σ., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 625e}}; κινδυνεύω πεπονθέναι ὅπερ.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Grg.” 485e}}: with _future_ _infinitive_, uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.117}}; κινδυνεύει _impersonal_, **it may be, possibly**, as an affirmatative answer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 256e, “Phdr.” 262c}}; out of courtesy, when no real doubt is implied, κινδυνεύεις ἀληθῆ λέγειν you **may very likely** be right, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 205d.}}
**5** _passive_, **to be endangered** or **imperilled**, ἐν ἑνὶ ἀνδρὶ πολλῶν ἀρετὰς κ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.35; τὰ χρήματα κινδυνεύεται τῷ δανείσαντι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.28}}:— but _passive_ in sense of _active_ uncertain in {{Refs|“GDI” 3569.4 (from Calymna).}}

2794:
** κίνδυν-ος**, ὁ,
irregular _dative_ κίνδῡνι (as if from κίνδυν) {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 138, compare 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 161}}:— **danger, hazard, venture**, whether abstract or concrete, πᾶσίν τοι κ. ἐπ᾽ ἔργμασιν {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 585, compare 637; ὁ μέγας κ. ἄναλκιν οὐ φῶτα λαμβάνει 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.81}}; κ. γαλέης **danger of** or **from** her, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 9; κ. ἀϋτᾶς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 9.35; τὸν κ. τῆς μάχης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.71}}; κίνδυνον ἀναρριπτέειν to run **a risk**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.50, etc.; ῥῖψαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 154; κἀνὰ κίνδυνον βαλῶ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1033}}; κίνδυνον ἀναλαβέσθαι, ὑποδύεσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.69, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.5.12}}; αἴρεσθαι, ἄρασθαι, ἀρεῖσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 504, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.63, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.11}}; ξυνάρασθαι Th.[same place]; ἐγχειρίσασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 5.108, etc.; ὑπομεῖναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.1; μετὰ τοῦ δικαίου ποιούμενος τοὺς κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.42; κινδύνῳ περιπίπτειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.27}}; ἐν κινδύνῳ αἰωρεῖσθαι, εἶναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 7.77, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.7; ἐπὶ κινδύνους χωρεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.39; πρὸς αὐθαιρέτους κ. ἰέναι [prev. author] 8.27; ἐς κ. ἐμβαίνειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.15; ἐς κ. καταστῆσαί τινα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.99; κινδύνῳ βάλλειν τινά 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1053; τὸν ἐπιόντα κίνδυνον 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.148; τοὺς ἐπιφερομένους ἑαυτῷ κ. [prev. work]163; τὸν κατειληφότα κ. τὴν πόλιν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.220; οὐ περὶ τῶν ἴσων ὁ κ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.7; ἔνι κ. ἐν τῷ πράγματι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 348}}; κ. [ἐστι] with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.21, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.27, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 436b, etc.; πόλιν κ. ἔσχε πεσεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 5; κ. ἀσφαλέστερος 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.9; κ. ἀνθρώπινοι.., θεῖοι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.139}}; ἐπὶ τῷ αὑτοῦ κ. at his own **risk**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1286a14; ἰδίῳ ἡμῶν κ. “PLond.” 2.356.4 (1st c.AD)}}; καθαρὸς ἀπὸ παντὸς κ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PIand.” 35.10 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**2** **trial, venture**, κ. ἀνεῖται σοφίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 955.}}
**3** **battle**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.87.10, etc.}}

2795:
** κῑνέω**,
_aorist_ ἐκίνησα, _Epic dialect_ κίνησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.730, etc.:}} —_middle_ and _passive_, _future_ κινήσομαι (in _passive_ sense) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 182c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.51, -ηθήσομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 796, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 545d}}, etc.: _aorist_ _middle_ (_Epic dialect_) κινήσαντο {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 2.582}}: _aorist_ _passive_ ἐκινήθην, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐκίνηθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.280: (compare κίω)}}:—**set in motion**, ἄγε κινήσας, of Hermes leading the souls, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.5}}; simply, **move**, οὐδέ τι κινῆσαι μελέων ἦν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8.298; κ. θύρην 22.394; κ. κάρη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.442, etc.; Ζέφυρος κ. λήϊον 2.147; κ. ὄμμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 866; ναῦς ἐκίνησεν πόδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 940 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.; σκληρὰ ἡ γῆ ἔσται κινεῖν, i.e. plough, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 16.11}}; κ. δόρυ, of a warrior about to attack, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 607; κ. στρατιάν [prev. author] “Rh.” 18 (_anapaest meter_); κ. ὅπλα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.82}}; κ. σκάφην **rock** a cradle, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 36 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**b** in later Gr., **set in motion** a process of law, etc., {{Refs|“PKlein.Form.” 405, etc.}}
**2** **remove** a thing from its place, ἀνδριάντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.183; γῆς ὅρια 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 842e}}; κ. τι τῶν ἀκινήτων **meddle with** things sacred, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.134, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1061, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.98}}; κ. τὰ χρήματα ἐς ἄλλο τι **apply** them to an alien purpose, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.24; κ. τῶν χρημάτων [prev. author] 1.143, 6.70; κ. τὸ στρατόπεδον 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.27, etc. (κινεῖν alone, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.54.2, cf. LXX.Gen.20.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Dio” 27)}}; **change, innovate**, νόμαια {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.80; τοὺς πατρίους νόμους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1268b28; τῶν κειμένων νόμων 7th c.BC: Zaleucus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.2.19}}:—_passive_, νόμιμα κινούμενα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 797b; ἰατρικὴ κινηθεῖσα παρὰ τὰ πάτρια 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1268b35}}: so _absolutely_ in _active_, **change treatment**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1286a13.}}
**3** Grammars, **inflect**, τὰ ῥήματα ἐκίνει τὸ τέλος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 104.15:}} —more usually in _passive_, κατὰ τὸ τέλος κινεῖσθαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 104.10.}}
**4** **alter** a manuscript reading, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 7.3.4.}}
**II** **disturb**, of a wasps' nest, τοὺς δ᾽ εἴ πέρ τις.. κινήσῃ ἀέκων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.264}}; **arouse**, κ. τινὰ ἐξ ὕπνου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 690}}; **urge on**, φόβος κ. τινά {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 289}}; φυγάδα πρόδρομον κινήσασα **having driven** him in headlong flight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 109 (Lyric poetry)}}; κ. ἐπιρρόθοις κακοῖσιν **attack, assail**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 413; μήτηρ κ. κραδίαν, κ. δὲ χόλον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 99 (_anapaest meter_); ἐάν με κινῇς καὶ ποιήσῃς τὴν χολὴν.. ζέσαι 4th c.BC: Anaxippus Comicus 2}}; κ. τινά **incite** or **stir** one **up** to speak, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 329e, “Ly.” 223a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.2}}; κ. τὰ πολλὰ καὶ ἄτοπα **stir up**.. questions, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 163a}}; **call in question** an assumption, τὰ μέγιστα κ. τῶν μαθηματικῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 271b11, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 27; κ. τὸ τὰ ἄκρα.. ἀνταίρειν 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.1.12, compare 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.1.6; ὁ κινῶν [τὰ φαινόμενα] λόγος 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 8.360}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1526}}; κινεῖται γὰρ εὐθύς μοι χολή my bile **is stirred**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 69.5; κεκινῆσθαι πρός τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 8.1.}}
**II.2** **set going, cause, call forth**, φθέγματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 18; πατρὸς στόμα [prev. author] “OC” 1276; μῦθον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 302; λόγον περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 450a; πάντα κ. λόγον [prev. author] “Phlb.” 15e; κ. ὀδύνην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 974 (_anapaest meter_); κακά [prev. author] “OT” 636; πάθος 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Mus.” p.4}} K.; πόλεμον, πολέμους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 566e; Ἐμπεδοκλέα.. πρῶτον ῥητορικὴν κεκινηκέναι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 65.}}
**II.3** in Medicine texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|κ. οὔρησιν, οὖρα, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.109, 127; κοιλίαν [prev. work]6.}}
**II.4** obscene meaning, κ. γυναῖκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 233.3 (nisi to be read ἐβίνουν),compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1052 (variant), “Eq.” 364, “Nu.” 1103 (Lyric poetry, _passive_), +others, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.7 (from Nicander)}}; κ. τὰ σκέλεα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.2.}}
**II.5** phrases: κ. πᾶν χρῆμα **turn** every stone, **try** every way, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.96}}; μὴ κ. εὖ κείμενον 'let sleeping dogs lie', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 15c}}; μὴ κίνει Καμάριναν, ἀκίνητος γὰρ ἀμείνων Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Stephanus Byzantius}}; κινεῦντα μηδὲ κάρφος 'not **stirring** a finger', {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.67, compare 1.55; μηδ᾽ ὀδόντα κινῆσαι [prev. author] 3.49}}; κ. τὸν ἀπ᾽ ἴρας πύματον λίθον 'play the last card', {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 82 (assuming variant).}}
**II.6** in Law, πολιτικῶς κ. κατά τινος **employ** civil **action** against, {{Refs|“Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 4.20.13.1.}}
**B** _passive_, **to be put in motion, go**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.47}}; <κι>νηθεὶς ἐπῄει uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 101}}: generally, **to be moved, stir**, κινήθη ἀγορή, ἐκίνηθεν φάλαγγες, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.144, 16.280}}; of an earthquake, Δῆλος ἐκινήθη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.98, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.8; θύελλα κινηθεῖσα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1660}}; τί κεκίνητα; what **motion is** this? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1226 (_anapaest meter_)}}; κινεῖσθαι, opposed to ἑστάναι, **motion**, opposed to rest, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 250b, etc.}}; ὥσπερ χορδαὶ ἐν λύρᾳ συμπαθῶς κινηθεῖσαι **vibrating** in unison, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.4.8.}}
**B.2** of persons, **to be moved, stirred**, ὁ κεκινημένος **one who is agitated, excited**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 245b, compare 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 45.25, +others; κ. παθητικῶς 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.193 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**B.3** of dancing, κ. τῷ σώματι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 656a.}}
**B.4** **move forward**, of soldiers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1371, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 139, “Ph.” 107}}; but κ. ἐκ τῆς τάξεως **leave** the ranks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.1.22.}}
**B.5** **to be disturbed** or **in rebellion**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 39.54, 42.15, +others}}
**B.6** κεκινημένος περί τι, Latin _versatus in_.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 908d.}}

2796:
** κίνησις** [ῑ], εως, ἡ,
**motion**, opposed to rest (στάσις), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 250a}}; opposed to ἠρεμία, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 202a5, etc.}}
**2** in Cyrenaic philosophical, λεία κ., ={ἡδονή}, τραχεῖα κ., ={πόνος}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.86; also αἱ διὰ μορφῆς κατ᾽ ὄψιν ἡδεῖαι κ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 67}}; αἱ κ. αἱ ἀνθρωπικαί human **emotions**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.20.19.}}
**3** **dance**, Ἄρεος κίνασις (sic) {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 16, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 63, “Ephes.” 2 No.71; τραγικὴ ἔνρυθμος κ. “Inscription Magn.” 165.}}
**4** **movement**, in a political sense, ἐν κ. εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.75, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.4.12}}; ἡ κ. ἡ Ἰουδαϊκή the Jewish **revolt**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“OGI” 543.15 (Ancyra, 2nd c.AD)}}; of the Peloponn. war, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1.}}
**5** **change, revolution**, κινήσεις πολιτείας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1268b25.}}
**6** **movement** of an army, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 10.23.1 (_plural_); πολεμικαὶ κ. 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 3.4, compare 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Tactica” 20.1.}}
**6.b** **removal, change of abode**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 97.17 (_plural_), +others}}
**7** Grammars, **inflexion**, τοῦ ζῆμι κ. οὐχ εὕρηται {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 410.38.}}
**8** in Law, **punitive action**, βασιλικὴ κ. {{Refs|“Codex Justinianus [4th-6th c.AD]” 1.3.43.10}}, compare 10.27.2.7; also, **setting** a process **in motion,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|PLond. 5.1663.13 (6th c.AD).}}

2798:
** κλάδος** [ᾰ], ου, ὁ,
**branch, shoot** of a tree, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Juventute” 468b25, “GA” 752a20}}; **twig**, opposed to ἀκρεμών, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.1.9, 1.10.7}}: generally, **branch**, τῆς ἐλαίης τοὺς κλάδους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.19}}: presented by suppliants, ἐλαίας θ᾽ ὑψιγέννητον κλάδον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 43, compare “Supp.” 22 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 3, 143}}; also of **laurel branches** used in temples, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 80.}}
**2** **plank,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|POxy. 1738.4, +others (3rd c.AD).}}
**3** **branch** of a blood-vessel, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.141.}}
**4** _metaphorically_, ἀπὸ νώτοιο δύο κλάδοι ἀΐσσονται two **arms**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 29.1.}}
**5** κ. ἐλέας, of a young girl, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“epigram Gr.” 368.7:—metapl. forms, _dative_ κλαδί “Scol.” 9, probably in “SIG” 1025.33 (Cos, 4th-3rd c.BC)}}; τῇ κ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 4.38 codices (compare 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 58.37); τῷ κ. 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 1.138; _accusative_ κλάδα “Lyrica Adespota” 122}}; compare κλάδα[ν]· κλάδον, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; _genitive_ _plural_ κλαδέων probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Philoxenus Epigrammaticus 1.3; _dative_ _plural_ κλάδεσι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 239 (Lyric poetry)}}, _Epic dialect_ κλαδέεσσι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 74.19; _accusative_ κλάδας [prev. work]53.}}

2799:
** κλαίω**,
old _Attic dialect_ **κλάω** (see. below) [ᾱ] never contracted; _Aeolic dialect_ **κλαΐω** {{Refs|“Lyrica Adespota” 65}}; _Epic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ _optative_ κλαίοισθα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.619: _Attic dialect_}}_imperfect_ ἔκλᾱον, _Epic dialect_ κλαῖον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.201}}, _Ionic dialect_ κλαίεσκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.364, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.119, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 312:}}_future_ κλαύσομαι, _2nd pers._ _singular_ κλαύσῃ or κλαύσει, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.340, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1327 (Lyric poetry), “Nu.” 58, 933 (_anapaest meter_), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 554, etc., rarely κλαυσοῦμαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1081, 1277 (in mock heroic verses)}}; _Attic dialect_ also κλαιήσω {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Dem.fragment” 10, κλᾱήσω 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.310, 21.99, later κλαύσω 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 23.34, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.70, NT.John.16.20, 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 3.143:}}_aorist_ ἔκλαυσα, _Epic dialect_ κλαῦσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.261}}:—_middle_, _aorist_ ἐκλαυσάμην {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 153, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.412 (7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus Mess.)}}:—_passive_, _future_ κλαυσθήσομαι {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Psa.77(78).64, κεκλαύσομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1436}}: _aorist_ ἐκλαύσθην [{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 831, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 8.11.1 (variant{κλαυθείς}), “IG” 14.2128}}]: _perfect_ κέκλαυμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 687, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1490, κέκλαυσμαι 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 273, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.115b. [κλάω [ᾱ]}} is recognized as _Attic dialect_ by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Adverbiis;” 187.26}}, and is found in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 341, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 792a, “Phlb.” 48a: ἔκλᾰε in later poetry, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.32, uncertain in 4th-3rd c.BC: Hermesianax Elegiacus 7.33 (compare κλέω A).] (κλᾰϝ-ψω, compare κλαυ-θμός, etc.)}}
**I** _intransitive_, **cry, wail, lament**, of any loud expression of pain or sorrow, κλαῖον δὲ λιγέως {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.201; πρὸς οὐρανόν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.364; τῆς ἄρα κλαιούσης ὄπα σύνθετο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.92}}; for the dead, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.297, etc.; ἀμφὶ δὲ σὲ Τρῳαὶ καὶ Δαρδανίδες κλαύσονται 18.340; κλαίοντα καὶ ὀδυρόμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 388b, etc.}}; διὰ τί οἱ κλαίοντες ὀξὺ φθέγγοντα; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 900a20; δάκρυσι κ. 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 59.27}}; of infants, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.107, +others}}; of **crying** for joy, κλαῖον δὲ λιγέως, ἁδινώτερον ἤ τ᾽ οἰωνοί κτλ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.216, compare 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1799.57.}}
**I.2** αὐτὸν κλαίοντα ἀφήσω {{Refs|1}} shall send him home **crying, howling**, i.e. **well beaten**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.263: frequently in _Attic dialect_, κλαύσεται}} **he shall howl**, i.e. **he shall suffer for it**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1327 (Lyric poetry), “Pl.” 174, +others; κλαύσομαι [prev. author] “Nu.” 58}}; κλαύσει μακρά you shall **howl** loudly, i.e. suffer severely, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pax” 255, compare 1277; κλαύσῃ φιλῶν τὸν οἶνον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 554; κλάοις ἄν, εἰ ψαύσειας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 925}}; κλαίων **to your sorrow** or **loss, at your peril**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 401, 1152, “Ant.” 754; κλάων ἅψῃ τῶνδε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 270, compare “Hipp.” 1086; δεῦρ᾽ ἔλθ᾽ ἵνα κλάῃς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 58}}; κλάειν ἔγωγέ σε λέγω (opposed to χαίρειν σοι λέγω) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pl.” 62, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.127; κλάειν εἴπωμεν 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 363; κλάειν κελεύων Λάμαχον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1131; κλάειν σε μακρὰ κελεύσας [prev. author] “Eq.” 433}}; σέ δ᾽ ἐᾶν κλάειν μακρὰ τὴν κεφαλήν **suffer** terribly in the head, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pl.” 612 (_anapaest meter_), compare “V.” 584.}}
**II** _transitive_, **weep for, lament**, κλαῖεν ἔπειτ᾽ Ὀδυσῆα, φίλον πόσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.363, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.210}}; τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 890, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1117; τὰ αὑτοῦ πάθη 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alcibiades” 33}}:— _passive_, **to be mourned** or **lamented**, ἀνδρὸς εὖ κεκλαυμένου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 687: _impersonal_, μάτην ἐμοὶ κεκλαύσεται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1436.}}
**II.2** **cry for**, of infants, μάμμας καὶ τιτθάς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.16.39.}}
**III** _middle_, **bewail oneself, weep aloud**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 920 (Lyric poetry):}}_perfect_ _participle_ _passive_ κεκλαυμένος **bathed in tears**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ch.” 457 (Lyric poetry), 731, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1490.}}
**III.2** _transitive_, **bewail to oneself**, πάθη.. πόλλ᾽ ἔγωγ᾽ ἐκλαυσάμην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 153; κλαιόμενα τάδε βρέφη σφαγάς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1096 (Lyric poetry).}}
